\par\par \b \uc2 Romanian Leu Seen Having Limited Depreciation Potential -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2023\par 15:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0904 ET - \uc2 Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to leave interest rates unchanged and deliver a relatively cautious tone at its next meeting on Thursday but any leu declines will be modest, ING says. The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solid track record of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the leu means any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses look limited, ING analysts say in a note. "Record demand for Romanian government bonds is also having a positive impact on the Romanian leu, which has been below the NBR's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level most of the time since the beginning of the year." EUR/RON rises 0.1% to 4.9030. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 ET - Elevated home prices and the runup in mortgage rates is weighing on demand for houses, says government-sponsored housing-finance company \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2 . "Consumer sentiment toward the housing market remains subdued," says \uc2 Doug Duncan\uc2 , \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2 's chief economist. "Until we see improvements in affordability via lower home prices and mortgage rates, we expect home sales to remain muted in the coming months." He says the company's latest Home Purchase Sentiment Index shows that most consumers expect home prices to fall or remain flat over the next year, which could prompt them to delay purchases. (will.feuer@wsj.com; @WillFOIA)\par \par \uc2 0901 ET - The end of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's monetary policy tightening will likely constitute a major turning point for global markets, \uc2 MFS Investment Management\uc2  says, expecting improving demand for risk assets. "The appetite for risk has been poor in recent months, but that may change in the first half of the year as central banks, including the Fed, signal a policy pause," MFS IM says. The asset manager believes that the upside to rates is now more limited, which has positive implications for fixed income. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 ET - Fixed income valuation has improved considerably over the past few quarters, making the asset class attractive again, MFS IM says in a note. "We are now observing levels of yield not seen in a decade," it says. This is also true for credit spreads, which have widened, but even more so for total yields which have benefited from the combined effects of the rate and spread widening, the asset manager says. From a strategic standpoint, the higher yields offer attractive opportunities as entry points in fixed income considering the historically high correlation of starting yields to subsequent returns, it says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 ET - Mexico produced 280K cars and light trucks in January, a 2.4% increase over the year-earlier month, statistics institute Inegi reports. Exports rose 9.9% from January 2022 to 238K units, and domestic new-car sales were up 20% at 94K units. Auto industry association AMIA projects production in 2023 to increase 6.6%, and exports to grow 7.2%. In 2022, production rose 9.2% from the previous year, and exports were up 5.9%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 ET - Canada logged only its second annual goods-trade surplus in recent years in 2022, widening to C$20.1B from a C$4.6B surplus in 2021. That followed yearly merchandise-trade deficits from 2015 to 2020. Last year saw a big jump in exports, up 22.1% thanks in large part to strength in prices, while imports rose 19.8%, with higher prices also playing a significant role, \uc2 Statistics Canada\uc2  says. Goods exports in 4Q22 fell 2.5%, a second consecutive quarterly drop, led by lower energy products, while imports were down 1.4%, the first decline since 2Q20. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0825 ET - Treasury yields give up some of their post-jobs report gains, ahead of Chair Powell's speech later today and as investors seem to scramble to guess when the Fed will pivot. Powell will speak at the economic club in Washington, his first chance to address Friday's surprisingly high employment gains. "With investors pricing in a significantly more hawkish policy response, all eyes will be on [Powell's] interview," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analysts say in a note. "Clearly any implication that there are upside risks to the Fed's rate outlook would validate the shift in market pricing over the last couple of days." The two-year yield is at 4.437% and the 10-year at 3.732%. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0821 ET - The U.K.'s 4Q 2022 GDP is expected to flatline with December's GDP shrinking to a more sizeable 0.4% month-on-month, driven by broad-based weakness across the construction, manufacturing and services sectors, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analyst Sanjay Raja says in a note. Although 2022 GDP growth is expected at around 4.1% the U.K. is still on course for a 2023 technical recession with activity expected to fall in the first two quarters, Raja says. Expectations are for 2023 GDP shrinking by 0.5% and expanding by 0.8% next year, with GDP crossing its pre-pandemic peak in 4Q, 2024, he says. "While the U.K. remains firmly on course for a negative growth year in 2023, we now think that the oncoming recession will be shorter and shallower," Raja says. (anthony.orunagoriainoff@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 ET - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  could cut the bank rate as soon as in the last quarter of 2023 as the U.K.'s economic activity slows, rate of unemployment rises, and inflation falls, UBS Research economist \uc2 Dean Turner\uc2  says in a note. "With the economy slowing, inflation falling, and unemployment rising, we expect the BOE to unwind some of the policy tightening from the past 10 meetings," he says. Turner expects the BOE base rate to peak at 4.25% in March. "With labour market data still signalling tightness, the BOE will likely deliver another small--25 basis points--hike in March, lifting base rates to 4.25%," he says. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 ET - The euro's downward correction against the dollar is likely to prove limited, \uc2 Argentex\uc2  says. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's modest 25 basis points interest rate rise last Wednesday lifted EUR/USD above 1.10 but the exchange rate suffered a correction after Friday's strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls report prompted investors to close out short dollar positions that bet on its fall, \uc2 Argentex\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Joe Tuckey says in a note. However, the data merely prolongs, not cancels, the gradual erosion of dollar strength, he says. "We think deeper pullback toward [EUR/USD] 1.05, if it were to happen, would be met with buyers," he says. "Trendline support at 1.0680 may arrest the downside move first." EUR/USD falls 0.2% to 1.0707. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 ET - U.K. house prices were flat in January and on-year growth slowed, according to new data from Halifax, as demand was burdened by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's consecutive rate rises, \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  says. However, looking ahead, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could pause or even cut rates later this year, which is good for the housing market, particularly if the economic downturn is less devastating than feared, Interactive head of investment Victoria Scholar says in a market comment. "The U.K. housebuilders suffered painful stock market losses last year but shares in \uc2 Taylor Wimpey\uc2  and \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  are both up by around 15% so far in 2023, reflecting the prospect we could be approaching the peak for U.K. interest rates," Scholar says. \uc2 Barratt Developments\uc2  is down 0.9%, \uc2 Taylor Wimpey\uc2  is up 0.1% and \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  is up 0.7%. (joseph.hoppe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0603 ET - The euro versus sterling saw a sharp increase in negative sentiment among European retail investors before the last interest rate decisions from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of England, Spectrum Markets says. Spectrum's SERIX investor sentiment indictor for derivatives linked to EUR/GBP fell further into negative territory before the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and BOE both raised rates by 50 basis points on Feb. 2. "While the eventual rate rises themselves were pretty widely anticipated by markets, there was a significant amount of volatility in the lead up to this which created opportunities for investors to act on short-term price movements, while the euro gained strength overall," Spectrum's Michael Hall says in a note. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 07, 2023 09:04 ET (14:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230207ej27002kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw94\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1410\pichgoal600
