\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Romania Outlook To Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2019\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 10 Dec 2019 16:57 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Romania Outlook To Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- Large spending deviations by the previous government have forced \par Romania's current leadership to revise up its fiscal targets for 2019 and \par 2020, against the backdrop of a slowing economy. \par      -- Planned wage and pension increases will contribute to a widening of \par Romania's already substantial current account deficit through 2020. \par      -- While we assume significant fiscal consolidation commences next year, \par the rigid budget structure and volatile policy environment pose risks to that \par assumption. \par      -- We are therefore revising our outlook on Romania to negative from \par stable, and affirming our 'BBB-/A-3' ratings. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Dec. 10, 2019, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on Romania to \par negative from stable. At the same time, we affirmed our long- and short-term \par foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings at 'BBB-/A-3'. \par  \par As a "sovereign rating" (as defined in EU CRA Regulation 1060/2009 "EU CRA \par Regulation"), the ratings on Romania are subject to certain publication \par restrictions set out in Art. 8a of the EU CRA Regulation, including \par publication in accordance with a pre-established calendar (see "Calendar Of \par 2019 EMEA Sovereign, Regional, And Local Government Rating Publication Dates: \par Midyear Update," published July 2, 2019, on RatingsDirect). Under the EU CRA \par Regulation, deviations from the announced calendar are allowed only in limited \par circumstances and must be accompanied by a detailed explanation of the reasons \par for the deviation. In this case, the reason for the deviation is the rising \par risk that delayed policy response could lead to the erosion of Romania's key \par credit strengths: its modest public and external leverage. The next scheduled \par rating publication on Romania will be in 2020, with the exact timing publicly \par available in our next "Calendar Of Sovereign, Regional, And Local Government \par Rating Publication Dates," which will be published later this month. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The outlook revision reflects increasing risks to Romania\u194\'00\'C2\u180\'00\'B4s economic and \par fiscal stability should policymakers be unsuccessful stabilizing and \par consolidating Romania's budgetary stance, including from plans to implement \par further pension hikes from next year. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower our ratings on Romania within the next 24 months if: \par      -- Fiscal and external imbalances continue to deteriorate and persist for \par longer than we currently anticipate, with the absence of fiscal consolidation \par resulting in higher public and external debt than we currently forecast. \par      -- A lack of economic policy synchronization leads to an overextension of \par real wages and increased exchange rate volatility, with potential negative \par repercussions on public- and private-sector balance sheets. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could revise the outlook to stable if we observed that the government has \par made headway in anchoring fiscal consolidation, leading to a stabilization of \par Romania's public finances and external position. \par  \par RATIONALE \par Policymakers have increased their projections for the budgetary deficit for \par 2019 and 2020 to 4.3% and 3.5%, respectively, of GDP from 2.8% and 3.0%, \par signifying a major deviation from original targets. These revisions largely \par reflect Romania's new government's transparent decision to settle overdue \par invoices and arrears with suppliers within the 2019 budget while revising \par downward its expectations for revenue growth. \par  \par However, we believe there are grounds to question the realism of the revised \par budgetary targets over the 2020-2022 period. First, we understand the \par government intends to stand by the pension hikes mandated in the July 2019 \par pension law, leading to over 3% of GDP in cumulative pension spending \par increases in 2020-2022. \par  \par Second, in our view, any plans for offsetting budgetary measures are \par complicated by the upcoming election cycle. Given that the general government \par deficit will likely exceed the EU's 3% of GDP Excessive Deficit Procedure \par (EDP) ceiling this year and next, Romania may not be able to avoid the EDP \par determined by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2   under the Stability and Growth Pact. \par Romania was last subject to the EDP from 2009 to 2013. \par  \par Third, an economic slowdown would weigh on government revenues, leading to \par further budgetary pressures. Under our base-case macroeconomic scenario, we \par assume growth remaining close to 3% through 2022. \par  \par Despite these concerns, we have decided to affirm the rating given \par comparatively low stocks of general government and external debt. The \par affirmation also reflects Romania's EU membership, which benefits its \par institutional framework, in our opinion. Constraints to the ratings include \par low economic wealth, relatively weak administrative capacity, an unpredictable \par policy environment, and only average monetary flexibility relative to peers. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Short-term political considerations \par continue to guide policy \par      -- Economic growth in Romania should remain just below 4% this year \par before weakening in 2020-2021, since external demand and wage growth are \par slowing. \par      -- Erratic policy execution, low administrative capacity, corruption, and \par polarization weigh on our institutional assessment, which is nevertheless \par helped by Romania's EU membership. \par      -- We do not expect meaningful fiscal consolidation before the 2020 \par elections. \par  \par We believe that the volatile political and policy environment will constrain \par the interim government, headed by Mr. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ludovic Orban\f28 \uc2 \f2  , in delivering reliable \par fiscal consolidation ahead of next year's general elections. Should the 2020 \par budget proposal not achieve parliamentary support in the December budget vote, \par the prospect for snap elections would rise. The likelihood of this scenario \par and its timing are difficult to predict given the fluidity of Romanian \par politics. Ultimately, we believe that an election and a reset of parliamentary \par positions would likely be required to form a government with a sufficiently \par strong mandate to move ahead with a firmer consolidation agenda. \par  \par Romania's political environment remains subject to confrontational rhetoric \par and parliamentary infighting. Political dynamics are in flux because support \par for the historically dominant Social Democratic PSD party has been declining, \par culminating in the October 2019 no-confidence vote and the landslide \par re-election of centrist President Iohannis in November. Given the busy \par election calendar, we anticipate that short-term political objectives will \par continue shaping Romania's historically procyclical fiscal policy. \par  \par The Romanian government has repeatedly deviated from the medium-term \par objectives stipulated in the EU's Stability and Growth Pact. The planned \par headline deficits for 2019 and 2020 suggest a longer delay in responding to \par the imposition of an EDP than we had anticipated. That said, we continue to \par regard Romania's EU membership as an important policy anchor, and we believe \par the very likely imposition of the EDP in 2020 will ultimately serve to instill \par fiscal discipline--but not before a new legislative term has commenced. For \par these reasons, our base-case scenario forecasts show fiscal deficits will \par start declining from 2021, after the general elections take place. \par  \par Romania will likely close 2019 with headline growth of just under 4%, after \par consumption-led expansion. This follows average wage inflation so far this \par year of close to 15% alongside increased government that boosts demand. We \par understand that authorities plan to move ahead with a 7.2% increase in the \par minimum wage early in 2020, a measure that would likely contribute further to \par short-term overheating and an even wider current account deficit. \par  \par Over the medium term, however, we believe growth will decelerate as fiscal \par impetus subsides. For our base-case macroeconomic scenario, we assume growth \par remaining close to 3% through 2022, though we acknowledge that there are \par near-term uncertainties in either direction. \par  \par Over the longer term, Romania's growth prospects will remain constrained by \par the high net emigration of skilled labor and negative population growth. \par Structural reforms to address these issues appear to be lacking. High wage \par growth in recent years has not been accompanied by comparable increases in \par productivity, which has in part eroded the competitiveness of Romania's \par exports. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: External imbalances have widened, but low \par stocks provide scope for adjustment \par      -- We expect general government deficits will reach 4.3% of GDP in 2019 \par and 4.0% of GDP in 2020. \par      -- The funding mix of Romania's current account deficits is shifting, as \par foreign direct investment (FDI) and capital account flows decrease. \par      -- Despite the uncertain fiscal outlook, we expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   \par to keep inflation expectations under control and maintain adequate foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. \par  \par Romania's fiscal deficit is widening more rapidly than we originally \par projected. In its November budget revision, the new government has announced a \par deficit of 4.3% of GDP for 2019 compared with the planned 2.8% deficit of its \par predecessor. The deterioration stems in part from weaker-than-expected tax \par revenues but is primarily due to payment of arrears to suppliers. We believe \par pressures on Romania's budget will persist through 2022. The composition of \par the budget and structure of the deficit suggest imbalances are significant, \par with spending on wages and pensions now standing at well over 80% of tax \par revenue. We consider the fiscal choices made thus far particularly \par problematic, given the country's challenging demographics. \par  \par Weaker fiscal performance stems from the pre-electoral context, which \par constrains the interim government's room to maneuver. We have revised our \par forecasts to include in 2020 fiscal expenditures of 1% of GDP, related to the \par pension law and only partly mitigated by government's potential offsetting \par measure. For 2021, we assume the elections will provide the government with a \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Dec 2019 16:57 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Romania Outlook To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par mandate to change material elements of the pension increases anticipated in \par the July pension law. Absent such measures, we project that Romania's fiscal \par deficit will climb to about 6% of GDP by 2022, with government debt sharply \par higher as a share of GDP, implying an increase in interest expenses and \par further strain on its external position. \par  \par Yields on government securities showed fluctuations over 2018-2019, with an \par increase in the cost of government borrowings. This, in our view, reflects the \par country's higher financing needs and greater uncertainty regarding fiscal \par policy. The widening of government deficits in 2019-2020 has amplified \par short-term financing requirements. With mounting uncertainty on the prospects \par of fiscal stabilization, this situation could lead to a further rise in \par funding costs and a higher interest bill. Romania's government debt stood at \par 35% of GDP in 2018, with government liquid assets representing approximately \par 5% of GDP. The maintenance of this fiscal reserve buffer will continue to help \par limit refinancing risk, alleviating the effects on access to financing in the \par event that foreign investment starts dwindling. \par  \par Romania's current account deficits will likely remain elevated through 2022, \par mirroring the ongoing consumption-led growth and evolution of the government's \par fiscal position. Strong domestic demand and softening exports will result in \par deficits exceeding 5.0% of GDP on average over 2020-2021, in our base case. \par Perhaps more importantly, we observe that the current account deficit is \par increasingly covered by debt-financed inflows, since FDI and capital account \par flows are moderating. Compared with 2017, deficit coverage by these flows \par declined to around 70% in 2018 from over 90% and have reduced further in 2019 \par as the current account deficit widened. Moreover, we note a lack of foreign \par greenfield investments, suggesting that investors might be increasingly \par hesitant due to the rapid wage growth, lack of infrastructure development, and \par continuing political and policy uncertainties in Romania. This will likely \par keep Romania's external debt on an upward trajectory. \par  \par The leu's value is determined under a managed float in an inflation-targeting \par regime, with a target of 2.5%, plus or minus 1 percentage point. We forecast \par an inflation rate of 4.0% in 2019, and believe that the inflationary \par environment will keep the spotlight on monetary policy, due to the need to \par firmly anchor inflation expectations. Consumer price index-based data shows \par that the real effective exchange rate has been stable since the end of 2016. \par However, wage-adjusted real effective exchange indicators suggest \par overvaluation. \par  \par We have observed that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   (NBR) has countered \par depreciation pressures on the leu through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par market, in particular through November which saw a meaningful net outflow of \par reserves. Failure to anchor fiscal policies will pressure the leu, further \par complicating the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's mission. Over 2019, the NBR has been adamant \par that an exchange rate correction as a way to handle the external deficit is \par not on the table. We expect the NBR to uphold monetary policy credibility, \par anchor inflation expectations, and keep foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves at a solid \par level. We forecast a controlled exchange rate correction through 2021 in our \par base scenario as the outlook for fiscal consolidation remains uncertain. \par Elevated exchange rate volatility could have severe repercussions on public- \par and private-sector balance sheets, in particular because about half of \par Romania's government debt is denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Moreover, an \par estimated 40% of financial sector deposits are denominated in foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as well as roughly one-third of sectorwide loans.s \par  \par Romania's predominantly foreign-owned banking sector remains sound, in our \par view. The system's loan-to-deposit ratio declined to about 80% at year-end \par 2018 from its peak of 137% in 2008. Lending growth has remained positive for \par the past two years, with loans to households and loans denominated in Romanian \par leu increasing strongly, though this trend has recently decelerated. \par Nonperforming loans had dropped to less than 5% of total loans by Dec. 31, \par 2018, from over 21% at midyear 2014. Liquidity and solvency ratios remain \par strong, and banks have maintained their profitability despite low interest \par rates. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, Dec. 12, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, Dec. 12, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Oct. 10, 2019. An interactive version is \par also available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: Midyear 2019, July 25, 2019 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2018 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transition, March 15, 2019 \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2019: Eurozone Commercial Borrowing To Increase 1.6% In \par 2019, Feb. 22, 2019 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in t