\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Short-term bond prices rise, Poland underperforms\b0\par\par\uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2016\par 11:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Hungary's 3-year bonds draw demand after auction\par     * CHF loan conversion plans remain a worry in Poland,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Dinar firms, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again\par \par     By Sandor Peto and Ivana Sekularac\par     BUDAPEST/BELGRADE, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Short-term government\par bonds rose in Central Europe on Friday as higher yields on the\par region's paper compared to its western neighbours attracted\par investors, although a possible U.S. interest rate rise left the\par assets with an uncertain outlook.\par     Hungary's 3-year bonds traded at a yield of 1.33 percent at\par 0750 GMT, compared with -0.66 percent on Germany's corresponding\par paper, while the 10-year yield was flat at 2.99 percent.\par     The 3-year yield was down 7 basis points from levels before\par an auction on Thursday which drew huge demand.\par     "That bond is attractive as its yield had surged from 1.22\par percent (in the last month)," one Budapest-based trader said.\par     That rise in yields came despite ratings agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard and\par Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  lifting the country back into investment grade on Sept.\par 16 in a surprise move. \par     Romania's 3-year yield was bid lower by 6 basis points at\par 1.49 percent, while its 10-year yield rose 3 basis points.\par     Risks of a rise in Polish government spending and Warsaw's\par tension with Brussels over the rule of law have kept its bond\par yields underpinned, however. \par     They rose 1-2 basis points along the curve, with 10-year\par papers trading at 3.04 percent.\par     In both Poland and Romania, planned legislation on the\par conversion of Swiss franc mortgages partly at the cost of banks\par have also weighed on assets. Pressure on bonds is higher in\par Poland, Raiffeisen analyst Imre Stephan said in a note.\par     Poland's financial regulator KNF said on Thursday that\par Polish banks would face bigger than expected costs if a proposal\par to make them return excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conversion spreads\par charged to clients becomes law.\par     Concerns over a Romanian bill on conversions, which\par parliament is seen passing on Tuesday, knocked the leu \par down to 3-month lows early this week.\par     Rating agency Fitch said on Thursday that Romanian banks\par which have relatively large Swiss franc loan portfolios had set\par aside sufficient reserves for possible conversion losses.\par     The leu was flat at 0844 GMT. The Bucharest bourse's index\par  touched an 8-week low, underperforming regional indices\par which rose as higher than expected Chinese inflation data helped\par Asian and European stocks rebound.\par     The dinar firmed 0.1 percent to 122.97 against the\par euro, past levels where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold it in the market\par on Wednesday to prevent a strengthening. \par     The bank did not cut its 4 percent benchmark rate on\par Thursday, citing an uncertain monetary policy outlook in the\par world.\par     It could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again if the dinar does not stay in the\par 123-124 range, traders said.\par     Demand for the dinar has been boosted by mid-month VAT and\par wage payments, but it is also buoyed by the government's fiscal\par consolidation policies, traders said.    \par     \par            CEE MARKETS  SNAPSHOT   AT  1044 CET           \par                         \f28 \b \highlight22\