\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Czech crown firm, industrial output beats forecasts\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2015\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Crown up despite central banker comment on possible weaker\par cap\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rangebound, forint and zloty off multi-year\par highs\par     * Hungarian bond yields rise, tracking U.S., indicating\par caution\par \par  (Adds leu retreat, new prices and comments)\par     By Sandor Peto and Robert Muller\par     BUDAPEST/PRAGUE, April 10 (Reuters) - The crown \par gained on Friday after the Czech Republic reported industrial\par output had risen more than expected, another  sign growth in\par Central European economies is accelerating.\par     Czech industrial output rose 4.5 percent in annual terms in\par February, compared with analysts' 4 percent forecast.\par  The crown gained 0.1 percent against the euro,\par trading at 27.385 at 1202 GMT.\par     Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lubomir Lizal\f28 \uc2 \f2  said late on\par Thursday the probability had grown that the bank will shift its\par cap on the value of the crown from the current 27 per euro and\par set a weaker value for the crown. \par     But his comments were based on conclusions from a meeting in\par March and did not take into account data released this week,\par Komercni Banka chief economist Jan Vejmelek said in a note.\par     The forint and the zloty were off\par multi-year highs reached earlier this week. The forint \par firmed 0.4 percent to 297.33 and if it closes firmer than\par 297.50, it can firm to 295 on Monday, one trader said.\par     The yield on Hungary's 10-year benchmark government bonds\par rose 10 basis points to 3.25 percent, tracking the last days'\par rise in U.S. 10-year Treasuries yields, indicating caution.\par     Possible further monetary easing in the region may help\par short government debt maturities and weigh on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Analysts in a Reuters poll unanimously said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would keep its rates on hold at 1.5 percent when it meets\par next week. \par     The zloty firmed 0.2 percent versus the euro to\par 4.019.\par     Romania's leu eased 0.2 percent to 4.408, bucking\par the regional trend. It bounced back from 4.4, the level beyond\par which analysts have said the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could sell it\par to curb its strength.\par     But dealers said illiquid trade ahead of a three-day holiday\par caused the retreat rather than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par Markets in Serbia, Bulgaria and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will remain closed on\par Monday for their Orthodox Easter holidays.     \par     Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered its main rate on Thursday by\par 50 basis points to 7 percent. Raiffeisen analyst Stephan Imre\par said in a note that dinar-denominated bonds remained well bid\par after the rate cut and bets on more to come.\par     "Against this backdrop, further rate cut speculations should\par lend continued support to the dinar debt market going forward,\par which still offers the highest yield levels in the CE/SEE\par (Central and Southeast European) region," he added. \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1402 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************