\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Hungarian shares fall on Internet tax proposal as other bourses rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,011 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2014\par 15:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares fall 3 pct on Internet tax plan\par     * Unipetrol Q3 earnings boost its shares by 4 pct\par     * Dinar gains after Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Leu firms mildly after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells Eurobond\par \par  (Adds Hungarian party proposal and auction, and Serbian budget)\par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Oct 22 (Reuters) - Hungary bucked gains in Central\par European equities on Wednesday as a government proposal for a\par new tax on Internet traffic knocked down the shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar\par Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's biggest Internet services\par provider.\par     Hungarian Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viktor Orban\f28 \uc2 \f2 's government,\par re-elected with a strong majority in April, has cut personal\par income taxes in the past few years but imposed higher taxes on\par some corporate sectors with strong foreign ownership, including\par banks and telecommunications, unnerving international investors.\par     The draft 2015 tax bill filed to parliament late on Tuesday\par contains a new provision for Internet providers to pay 150\par forints ($0.60) per gigabyte of data traffic, although they\par would be able to offset corporate income tax against the\par Internet tax. \par     Hungary's ruling Fidesz party on Wednesday proposed setting\par a cap on the planned tax, but the move failed to lift the stocks\par of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par      The shares fell more than 3 percent after the market\par opening and were down 2.6 percent by 1234 GMT.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares eased 0.3 percent in Frankfurt,\par while the bourse's main stock index rose.\par     The new levy could increase \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 's tax burden by\par as much as 10.5 billion forints, the equivalent of 10 forints\par per share, Budapest-based brokerage Equilor said in a note\par before the Fidesz party proposal. \par     "In theory the company can pass the new burden on to\par customers but that would be a business policy decision," Equilor\par said, adding that the new tax also increased uncertainty over\par whether Telekom will pay a dividend for 2014.  \par     Budapest's main equities index fell by 0.3 percent,\par in contrast with moderate rises in most other Central European\par bourses, led by a 0.8 percent rise in Bucharest.\par     The Hungarian debt agency scrapped a one-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bill\par auction as demand was low after a decline in yields to record\par lows in the past months, and many investors hoped better prices\par at an 8-week bill auction on Monday.  \par     Prague's main stock index rose 0.2 percent.\par     The shares of Czech oil processing firm Unipetrol \par surged about 4 percent. The company posted a third-quarter net\par profit of 1.4 billion crowns ($65 million), after a loss a year\par ago, thanks to higher margins and rising sales. It had gained a\par bigger stake in the country's main refinery. \par     The shares of Unipetrol's main owner, Polish \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKN Orlen\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  rose 0.8 percent, outperforming the Warsaw bourse's\par main index which firmed 0.4 percent.\par     \par     SERBIA APPROVES BUDGET REVISION\par     Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were rangebound, except for the Serbian\par dinar which was firmer by 0.2 percent against the euro\par at 1234 GMT, after the Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par market to defend the dinar on Tuesday, selling 10 million euros.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gave up part of its early gains after the\par government raised its budget deficit target for the year to 5.7\par percent from 4.6 percent but said it had nonetheless agreed the\par "principles" of a new precautionary loan programme with the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its 8.5 percent interest rate, which\par is the highest in the region, on hold last week as uncertainty\par over talks with the IMF and the government's delay in adopting a\par plan to steady its finances kept the dinar weak. \par     Finance Minister Dusan Vujovic said Serbia would issue a\par Eurobond by the end of the year.   \par     The leu was flat versus the euro after Romania\par raised 1.5 billion euros ($1.9 billion) in a 10-year Eurobond\par issue on Tuesday. \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1434 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.690    27.598   -0.33%  -1.29% \par  Hungary forint             306.150   306.310   +0.05%  -3.00% \par  Polish zloty                 4.222     4.221   -0.02%  -1.71% \par  Romanian leu                 4.417     4.418   +0.01%  +0.97% \par  Croatian kuna                7.669     7.667   -0.03%  -0.67% \par  Serbian dinar              119.300   119.555   +0.21%  -4.02% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  *******************