\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1-Hungarian shares hit by Internet tax plan, other equity markets rise\b0\par\par\uc2 911 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2014\par 10:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares fall 3 pct on Internet tax plan\par     * Unipetrol Q3 earnings boost its shares by 4 pct\par     * Dinar gains 0.4 pct after Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Leu firms mildly after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells Eurobond\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Oct 22 (Reuters) - Hungary bucked gains in Central\par European equities on Wednesday as a government plan to impose a\par new tax on Internet traffic knocked down the shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar\par Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's biggest Internet services\par provider.\par     Hungarian Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Viktor Orban\f28 \uc2 \f2 's government,\par re-elected with a strong majority in April, has cut personal\par income taxes in the past few years but imposed higher taxes on\par some corporate sectors with strong foreign ownership, including\par banks and telecommunications, unnerving international investors.\par     The draft 2015 tax bill filed to parliament late on Tuesday\par contains a new provision for Internet providers to pay 150\par forints (60 US cents) per gigabyte of data traffic, although\par they would be able to offset corporate income tax against the\par Internet tax. \par     Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par , fell more than 3 percent after the market opening\par and were down 2.9 percent by 0737 GMT.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares firmed mildly in Frankfurt, but\par underperformed the bourse's main stock index.\par     The new levy could increase \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 's tax burden by\par as much as 10.5 billion forints, the equivalent of 10 forints\par per share, Budapest-based brokerage Equilor said in a note. \par     "In theory the company can pass the new burden on to\par customers but that would be a business policy decision," Equilor\par said, adding that the new tax also increased uncertainty over\par whether Telekom will pay a dividend for 2014.  \par     Budapest's main equities index fell by half a\par percent, in contrast with moderate rises in most other Central\par European bourses, led by a 0.9 percent rise in Bucharest's main\par index. \par     Prague's main index rose a quarter of a percent.\par     The shares of Czech oil processing firm Unipetrol \par surged 4 percent after it posted a third-quarter net profit of\par 1.40 billion crowns ($64.59 million), after a loss a year ago,\par thanks to higher margins and rising sales after it gained a\par bigger stake in the country's main refinery. \par     The shares of Unipetrol's main owner, Polish \f28 \uc2 \f2 PKN Orlen\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  rose 1.5 percent, outperforming the Warsaw bourse's\par main index which firmed a quarter of a percent.\par     \par     SERBIA APPROVES BUDGET REVISION\par     Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were rangebound, except for the Serbian\par dinar which firmed 0.4 percent against the euro,\par drifting further away from two-year lows.\par     The Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to defend\par the dinar on Tuesday, selling 10 million euros. The bank kept\par its 8.5 percent interest rate, which is the highest in the\par region, on hold last week as uncertainty over negotiations with\par the IMF and the government's delay in adopting a plan to steady\par its finances kept the dinar weak. \par     The Serbian government adopted a draft 2014 budget revision\par on Wednesday that is expected to contain cuts to pensions and\par public sector wages. It was expected to announce details later\par in the day. \par     The leu firmed mildly versus the euro after\par Romania raised 1.5 billion euros ($1.9 billion) in a 10-year\par Eurobond issue on Tuesday.       \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 0929 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.595    27.598   +0.01%  -0.95% \par  Hungary forint             306.300   306.310   +0.00%  -3.05% \par  Polish zloty                 4.220     4.221   +0.03%  -1.66% \par  Romanian leu                 4.416     4.418   +0.05%  +1.01% \par  Croatian kuna                7.666     7.667   +0.01%  -0.63% \par  Serbian dinar              119.110   119.555   +0.37%  -3.87% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par                                      close     change  in 2014 \par  Prague                       941.25   939.12   +0.23%  -4.63% \par  Budapest                   17411.64 17493.35   -0.47%  -5.03% \par  Warsaw                      2429.43  2423.29   +0.25%  +1.04% \par  Bucharest                   6991.84  6928.58   +0.91%  +7.93% \par  Ljubljana                    801.95   800.30   +0.21% +25.11% \par  Zagreb                      1836.08  1833.08   +0.16%  +3.13% \par  Sofia                        515.46   513.93   +0.30%  +4.90% \par  ***************************** BONDS **************************