\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2--Bonds firm, Romania keeps interest rates on hold\b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2014\par 17:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Romania keeps rates on hold, does not change reserve\par requirements\par     * Bonds advance cautiously, recovery offsets Ukraine impact\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, May 6 (Reuters) - Central European government\par bonds firmed slightly on Tuesday, helped by strong economic data\par from around the region that overshadowed concerns over the\par situation in neighbouring Ukraine's crisis.\par     Romanian bonds extended their recent gains, with 10-year\par yields dropping 2 basis points to 4.91 percent, benefiting from\par a regional downtrend in yields.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its main interest rate at a\par record low of 3.5 percent as expected. It also opted not to cut\par commercial banks' reserve requirements despite some expectations\par that it might do to boost liquidity in domestic markets.\par  \par     Rates are at record lows in some parts of the region as\par central banks try to support recoveries in their economies.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , however, manages liquidity via market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other tools rather than through its main rate.\par     "We believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will find ways to stimulate\par economic growth further, even without using its more visible\par instruments," said Vlad Muscalu, chief economist at ING Bank in\par Bucharest.\par     Governor Mugur Isarescu reaffirmed at a news conference that\par the bank would lower reserve requirements in "adequate" doses at\par the right time, after a surprise cut earlier this year. He also\par said the bank had cut its inflation forecasts slightly.\par  \par     \par     UKRAINE LESS OF A WORRY\par     Romania has seen the fastest economic recovery in central\par Europe, while inflation has been low throughout the region.\par     Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were supported, while the hunt\par for high-yielding debt in Europe in recent weeks buoyed debt\par markets and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds.\par     Hungary's assets outperformed their regional peers on\par Tuesday after data showing retail sales there surged 8.3 percent\par year on year in March, adding to signs that domestic consumption\par and economic growth is recovering.\par     The forint firmed 0.3 percent to 307.18 per euro. \par     The region's stock markets were mixed. Budapest's main\par equities index rose 1 percent, with drug maker Richter\par  shares rebounding, gaining 1.1 percent after a 3\par percent plunge on Monday.\par     Richter has strong sales bases in Ukraine and Russia. Its\par earnings, which it will report on Wednesday, are expected to be\par hurt by the fall of the rouble. \par     The markets largely turned their back on the conflict in\par Ukraine on Tuesday, although the country appears to be slipping\par ever closer towards war after the deadliest week since the\par separatist uprising began. \par     "The markets are mostly ignoring the conflict now, which may\par prove to be a mistake," one Budapest-based fixed-income trader\par said.\par     Hungarian bond prices reversed an early fall. Yields\par returned to their recent decline, and the 3-year benchmark yield\par  dropped 3 basis points to 4.05 percent.\par     Polish government bonds also firmed mildly ahead of the\par auction of a new series of 5-year paper on Thursday.\par     "There always tends to be new demand for new paper,\par especially in light of the low inflation," said Maciej Popiel\par from bank PKO \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1517 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.419    27.419   0.00%  -0.32%\par  Hungarian forint           307.180   308.060  +0.29%  -3.32%\par  Polish zloty                 4.202     4.204   +0.04%  -1.25%\par  Romanian leu                 4.438     4.438   0.00%  +0.50%\par  Croatian kuna                7.585     7.592   +0.09% +0.44%\par  Serbian dinar              115.560   115.500    -0.05%   -0.92%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      1005.28    1008.57   -0.33% +1.86%\par  Budapest                  17546.37  17373.54   +0.99%   -4.30%\par  Warsaw                     2393.20   2405.87    -0.53%  -0.46%\par  Bucharest                  6422.44   6401.39  +0.33%   -0.86%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (bid)    change   vs Bund   change in\par  Czech Republic                                      spread\par    2-year                0.314   +0.051   +16bps    +4bps\par    5-year                0.879    +0.002   +34bps      0bps\par   10-year                1.838    -0.009   +38bps     -1bps\par  Hungary\par    3-year                4.050     -3.000   +384bps    -301bps\par    5-year                4.380     -0.010   +384bps    -1bps\pa