\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , stocks mildly positive but Ukraine worries linger\b0\par\par\uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Forint leads \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains\par     * Polish GDP up 0.6 pct q/q, little reaction\par     * Hungary investments rose 14.9 pct in Q4\par \par     BUDAPEST, Feb 28 (Reuters) - Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par stocks opened flat to slightly stronger on Friday but traders\par said cautious sentiment would persist as investors kept an\par uneasy eye on developments in neighbouring Ukraine.\par     Hungary's forint led \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in early trade\par with a 0.3 percent gain versus the euro, tracked by the Polish\par zloty, the region's most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which added\par 0.2 percent by 0911 GMT.\par     Fourth-quarter Polish economic growth of 0.6 percent\par quarter-on-quarter and 2.7 percent year-on-year virtually\par matched flash estimates and had no market impact. Dealers did\par not expect much volatility unless tensions in Ukraine's Crimea\par region escalate further.\par     Data showed investments in Hungary rose by an annual 14.9\par percent in the fourth quarter following a 9.8 percent increase\par in the third quarter in a boon to central Europe's most indebted\par economy. \par     "The favourable interest rate environment and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National\par Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Funding for Growth Scheme have helped\par kick-start investments," the Economy Ministry said in a\par statement.\par     Raiffeisen Bank analyst Zoltan Torok said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par programme, which provides cheap loans to companies via\par commercial banks, would probably give private sector investments\par a further push in the coming quarters.\par     "We are bouncing up and down around the 310 per euro level.\par We are looking at Ukraine but right now there is some\par wait-and-see attitude by investors," a Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par dealer said.\par     "But if anything serious were to happen, we would also take\par a hit," the trader said. Another dealer said the forint would\par probably remain in a 309.5-311 range to the euro on Friday in\par low turnover.\par     Even though the region has thin trade links with Ukraine,\par the neighbouring country's crisis has sent a shockwave through\par the region's markets and debt default or a military conflict in\par Ukraine could deepen the slide in asset prices.\par     Contagion from Ukraine is the latest setback for central\par European markets, which have been hit this year by risk aversion\par as the U.S. Federal Reserve began slowing its money printing.\par     On Friday stock markets posted modest gains, led by a 1.4\par percent increase in Budapest shares. Stocks in central\par Europe's biggest independent lender OTP rebounded from\par Thursday's falls to gain 2 percent.\par     But a fall in shares in Erste Bank, which is also\par listed in Prague, dragged down stocks in the Czech Republic\par  after the lender earlier reported a drop in net profit.\par Erste and energy company CEZ are the biggest listed companies on\par the Prague exchange. \par     The Romanian leu, which has been backed by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was steady in early trade.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's President Traian Basescu may approve the latest\par review of an aid deal led by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par later on Friday.\par     Basescu had previously refused to approve the review of the\par precautionary, 4 billion euros aid deal because it included an\par additional tax on fuels and a government plan to support low\par income borrowers - policies he opposed.    \par     The finance ministry is also expected to unveil domestic\par debt issuance plans for March. Debt managers sold 1.93 billion\par lei ($586.91 million) of leu-denominated debt in February.\par     "As recent political tensions seem to have gone past the\par tipping point, an early RON firming to 4.50/EUR gained further\par steam to 4.49/EUR at the end of yesterday's session due to a\par rebound in the regional climate," INB Bank in Bucharest said in\par a note.\par     "The RON is nonetheless eyeing 4.50/EUR this morning and we\par still expect the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stick to softer ground up to\par 4.51/EUR today, with a rebound probable when the new government\par in the making will be announced on Monday," it said.\par     The Serbian dinar was unchanged after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold 10 million euros in Thursday's session.\par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1011 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.329    27.325   -0.01% +0.01%\par  Hungarian forint           309.680   310.700  +0.33%  -4.10%\par  Polish zloty                 4.159     4.167   +0.19%  -0.23%\par  Romanian leu                 4.496     4.497  +0.03%   -0.79%\par  Croatian kuna                7.649     7.653   +0.05%  -0.41%\par  Serbian dinar              115.830   115.920   +0.08%   -1.15%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\p