\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Leu gains as Central Europe's fundamentals prevail\b0\par\par\uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2014\par 15:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * C.bank's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week, IMF deal support leu\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rate easing cycle likely coming to an end\par     * Hungary T-bill demand surges\par \par     By Sandor Peto and Radu Marinas\par     BUDAPEST/BUCHAREST, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Romania's leu shrugged\par off an expected interest rate cut to lead Central European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher on Tuesday, as further signs the region's\par economy is rebounding from recession outweighed disappointing\par U.S. economic data. \par     The leu rose as much as 0.7 percent to the euro\par before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut rates by 25 basis points to\par a new record low of 3.5 percent, seen as the last of a\par 175-basis-point easing cycle which began in the middle of last\par year.\par     It edged up further after the move, which flouted a recent\par trend towards higher borrowing costs in emerging markets. Turkey\par and South Africa both raised rates last month to support their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during a massive emerging markets sell-off.\par     "The cut and its size was expected and the leu did not\par soften because it's being given support from money market rates\par following last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which trimmed\par liquidity," said one Bucharest-based dealer.\par     News that the Romanian government and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International\par Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  had reached agreement over a review of the\par country's 4 billion euro aid deal also lent the leu support.\par  \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped into the market last week\par to defend the leu, but bought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in much smaller\par amounts than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in other emerging economies. The leu\par has been Central Europe's most stable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year. \par     Romania postponed a tender on Monday to sell one-year bills,\par citing global market turbulence and saying that it had already\par met most of its 2014 borrowing needs. \par     Market participants said the leu, which was bid at 4.457\par against the euro, looked safe despite the rate cut.\par     "The unusually thin volumes coupled with discouragingly high\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap rates suggest the slight firming of the leu to beyond\par 4.49/EUR yesterday was likely fuelled by unwinding bets against\par the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said ING economist Mihai Tantaru said.\par     "Elevated short-term rates should entertain a similar\par trading pattern for the leu with 4.45/EUR the next short-term\par target," he added.\par     \par     HUNGARY DEBT DEMAND PICKS UP\par     Hungary sold more three-month treasury bills than planned on\par Tuesday as investors scrambled to shorten the maturity of their\par Hungarian debt portfolio, betting on a halt or reversal in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rate cuts.\par     Investors rushed to buy short-term paper before a bond\par expiry worth 439.5 billion forints ($1.9 billion) on Feb. 12. \par An uncertain interest rate outlook means keeping money in\par longer-dated debt is more risky. \par     The region's most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the zloty and\par the forint, rose about 0.6 and 0.3 percent\par respectively, regaining some ground after losses in the past\par weeks as a sell-off in other emerging markets spilled over into\par Central European markets.\par     "A series of positive data arrived from CEE, particularly\par Poland's strong PMI (Purchasing Manager Index)," said Piotr\par Poplawski, analyst of Bank BGZ in Warsaw. \par     "Add that to weak U.S. ISM (manufacturing survey data) and\par CEEs are on the rebound."\par     In recent months, better economic balance and growth\par prospects have mostly protected Central Europe from the jitters\par in other emerging markets, but in January investors began to\par ignore differences in economic fundamentals.\par     A rise in January PMIs in Central Europe helped its assets\par on Monday. The effect lasted even after weak economic data in\par the United States triggered an overnight plunge in equity\par markets around the globe.\par     "Stocks are falling in the region, too, but you see we can\par call that moderate compared to (more than 4 percent) fall in the\par Nikkei (of Japan)," one Budapest-based dealer said.\par     Bucharest's main index led the decline in regional\par equities, shedding 1.2 percent. Budapest dropped 0.3\par percent.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also indicated last week that it had\par room to reduce rates again after cutting them to 2.85 percent\par from 7 percent, where they stood in August 2012. \par     Hungary is the region's most indebted nation, and some\par investors are betting it and Russia will be the next emerging\par economies forced to defend their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by raising rates.\par     Russia's rouble hit its lowest level in nearly five years on\par Tuesday. The forint joined the sharp \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rebound in Central\par Europe, but investors were cautious.\par     "The Hungarian forint includes a considerable risk component\par regarding the uncertainty of ongoing interest rate cuts in our\par view," Raiffeisen said in a note on the region's markets.\par \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1316 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.518    27.560  +0.15%  -0.68%\par  Hungarian forint           310.750   311.800  +0.34%  -4.43%\par  Polish zloty                 4.213     4.238   +0.60%  -1.49%\par  Romanian leu                 4.456     4.486  +0.68%  +0.10%\par  Croatian kuna                7.659     7.650    -0.12%  -0.54%\par  Serbian dinar              115.850   115.800    -0.04%   -1.17%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      977.63    986.81   -0.93%  -0.94%\par  Budapest                  18537.21  18552.47    -0.08%  +1.11%\par  Warsaw                     2353.08   2369.57    -0.70%  -2.13%\par  Bucharest                  6361.48   6434.39   -1.13%   -1.80%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (bid)    change   vs Bund   change in\par  Czech Republic                                      spread\par    2-year                0.447   +0.016   +38bps    +3bps\par    5-year                1.157     -0.052   +52bps      -4bps\