\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Zloty holds steady, Czech crown off 3-month peak\b0\par\par\uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2013\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Zloty underpinned as BGK expected to sell euros\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Crown off peak hit after cbank opted against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says has covered 78 pct of 2013 funding needs\par     BELGRADE/BUCHAREST, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Potential support\par from a Polish state bank steadied the zloty on Friday while the\par crown pulled back from a three-month high reached after the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opted against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for now.\par     Traders expect the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be underpinned by\par sales of euros by state-controlled bank BGK before a fixing on\par Monday that will determine how much Poland has to pay to match\par European subsidies. \par     Two market sources told Reuters they have seen unusual\par activity from BGK in the past few days, something that could\par continue until 1215 GMT on Monday when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par carries out the fixing.\par     "These are not big sums but we are seeing them very often,\par which is not typical," said one of the sources.\par     The zloty was flat on the day at 4.228 per euro at\par 1320 GMT, while the crown had dropped 0.4 percent from\par Thursday's close to bid at 25.679 to the euro. \par     Czech rate setters voted on Thursday for the second meeting\par in a row against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the crown in order to\par ease monetary conditions further now that interest rates are\par near zero. \par     But the bank said it stood ready to use the tool if needed,\par which is a threat analysts and dealers say will put a cap on\par crown gains going ahead. \par     The crown was just off 25.575 per euro hit late on Thursday,\par its strongest since June, as it neared the 25.50 level seen as a\par line that could prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into action.\par     Dealers expect the crown to settle back into the range of\par 25.65-26.00 seen in recent months.     \par     "The possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is staying on the table\par (and) will limit gains in the crown. We could see verbal\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (if the crown is) around 25.50 in the coming\par weeks," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ceska Sporitelna\f28 \uc2 \f2  bank said in a note.\par     Elsewhere in the region, investors will keep their eye on\par interest rate decisions in Romania and Poland next week. \par     While Warsaw is seen holding rates at 2.50 percent,\par Bucharest, which started its easing cycle later due to stubborn\par inflation, is seen shaving another quarter point, bringing\par borrowing costs to a record 4.25 percent. \par     The Romanian leu was down 0.1 percent against the\par euro. The Hungarian forint rose 0.3 percent and yields\par regained some ground as as concerns over budget and debt\par negotiations in Washington weakened the dollar on Friday. \par     But concerns also kept a lid on gains in world shares and\par limited investors appetite for emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par     In Romania, the board of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par will discuss its letter of intent for a new two-year, 4 billion\par euro standby deal on Friday. Romania's treasury chief said the\par EU state has covered 78 percent of its funding needs and would\par tap the rest at domestic tenders.               \par             CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1542 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2013\par  Czech crown                 25.667    25.580   -0.34%  -2.41%\par  Hungarian forint           298.750   299.640  +0.30%  -2.61%\par  Polish zloty                 4.225     4.227   +0.05%  -3.57%\par  Romanian leu                 4.461     4.458   -0.08%   -0.41%\par  Croatian kuna                7.612     7.612    0.00%  -0.80%\par  Serbian dinar              114.530   114.490    -0.03%   -1.95%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2013\par  Prague                      959.60    956.60  +0.31%  -7.62%\par  Budapest                  18714.53  18729.87    -0.08%  +2.98%\par  Warsaw                     2420.72   2435.83    -0.62%  -6.28%\par  Bucharest                  6048.41   6069.17   -0.34%  +17.45%\par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                          Yield    Yield    Spread    Daily\par                          (bid)    change   vs Bund   change in\par  Czech Republic                                      spread\par    2-year                0.253   +0.006   +8bps    +3bps\par    5-year                1.173     -0.017   +37bps     +2bps\par   10-year                2.352    -0.027   +57bps    +2bps\par  Hungary\par    3-year                4.580     -0.050   +428bps    -2bps\par    5-year                5.070    +0.040   +427bps  