\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MONEY-Market turmoil cuts Romania's room for manoeuvre on IMF deal\b0\par\par\uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2013\par 13:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * IMF to review Romania's 5 bln euro aid deal this week\par \par \uc2 * Analysts expect long negotiations for successor deal\par \par \uc2 * Continued IMF help crucial for investment\par \par \uc2 By Luiza Ilie\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, June 24 (Reuters) - A rapid sell-off in emerging markets has left Romania's government with an awkward choice - push ahead quickly with politically sensitive reforms or risk the loss of an IMF safety belt and a run on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt.\par \par \uc2 Romania appeared to be in a comfortable position before the U.S. Federal Reserve rocked markets by signalling it will reduce monetary stimulus, sending developing country assets - including Romania's - tumbling.\par \par \uc2 A debt tender failed last week for the first time in almost a year, and the leu has lost roughly 5 percent against the euro since the start of May, the most of any central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A 5 billion euro \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 -led aid deal provides some guarantees for investors, even though Bucharest has not drawn on the funds. But it expires at the end of June and the government has still to reform inefficient state firms, sell assets and use more EU development funds.\par \par \uc2 While the IMF may broadly agree that Romania has met its commitments in the current deal given it stuck with austerity to bring the budget gap under 3 percent of national output, its lack of long-term reforms will complicate negotiations over a new backstop.\par \par \uc2 "This is a high risk aversion moment in the region and risks associated with a sudden stop of the IMF agreement are fairly high," said Mihai Patrulescu, senior economist at \uc2 Unicredit Tiriac Bank\uc2  in Bucharest.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said investors place great value on the presence of an IMF deal which gives them more confidence that the government will pursue reforms and maintain budget discipline.\par \par \uc2 DEAL REVIEW\par \par \uc2 Romania has had IMF-led aid deals since 2009 and has made large fiscal adjustments to exit the EU's excessive deficit procedure, a scrutiny process for the bloc's heavy spenders.\par \par \uc2 But subsequent governments have delayed privatisations and the overhaul of healthcare and infrastructure, while absorption of EU development funds has been weak, at roughly 15 percent. Local administrations are still struggling to pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 The IMF will likely need to see progress in these areas before agreeing a new deal, and the leftist cabinet of Prime Minister Victor Ponta may be reluctant to act swiftly as selling assets and raising healthcare costs would erode support.\par \par \uc2 Although the government has roughly 70 percent of parliament seats and remains popular in opinion polls, the four-party ruling coalition is relatively unstable and with a presidential election looming in 2014 it may be unwilling to sacrifice its support with unpopular measures.\par \par \uc2 Premier Victor Ponta and President Traian Basescu have both said they want a successor aid deal, as does \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mugur Isarescu who said it would provide safety in the face of volatile global markets. Still, some government officials have complained that some of the IMF's requests were unrealistic.\par \par \uc2 After selling loss making rail freight carrier \uc2 CFR Marfa\uc2  last week, Transport Minister Relu Fenechiu said the privatisation was done "at gunpoint" because of IMF deadlines. The lender first asked Romania to sell the carrier in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations may also be derailed by cabinet plans to raise army pensions, lower value added tax and hire more people in the public sector, all of which would pressure finances if enforced.\par \par \uc2 "Neither side will rush through negotiations," said Daniel Hewitt, emerging Europe economist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "But it is possible to work out a set of measures the government is willing to take and the IMF is willing to accept."\par \par \uc2 CREDIBILITY\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the IMF extended Romania's aid deal by three months, giving it more time to fulfil its commitments.\par \par \uc2 The cabinet has largely met those, culminating with the sale of a majority stake in \uc2 CFR Marfa\uc2 . It also sold a stake in gas grid operator Transgaz and picked advisers for several IPOs due later this year.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 CFR Marfa\uc2  sale illustrates Romania's struggles to reform companies that are still plagued by communist-era management techniques and inefficiency.\par \par \uc2 Two former bidders, who withdrew saying the short deadlines made it impossible to accurately assess the carrier, say they will file legal challenges against the sale, which still needs approval from the competition watchdog.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, the IMF has seen the agreement as a success, except when it comes to reforming state-owned enterprises," said Vlad Muscalu, a senior economist at ING Bank in Bucharest. "A third deal would aim to tackle that."\par \par \uc2 The government has a comfortable funding situation, having covered more than half its needs for 2013 and building a buffer fund that could cover the rest of the year. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains vulnerable and a sharp fall could compel the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soften any violent market moves.\par \par \uc2 Even more importantly, \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs the IMF's seal of approval to attract much needed investment. Foreign direct investment stood at 322 million euros in April, a sliver of Poland's 2.1 billion euro first-quarter foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 "Romania has a lot to do, it is so far behind," \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' Hewitt said. "Reforms are very delayed." (Reporting by Luiza Ilie; Editing by Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 ROMANIA-IMF/ (CEE MONEY) | ABN | D | E | RBN | BNX | M | RDN | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130624e96o000hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
