\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  up on US data, forint extends gains\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2013\par 15:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * PMI data props Hungarian forint\par \par \uc2 * Zloty strengthens after underperforming CEE\par \par \uc2 * Serbian dinar up on govt-related company subsidy\par \par \uc2 By Jason Hovet and Radu Marinas\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Feb 1 (Reuters) - Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Friday on benign US payroll data and the forint extended its three-day firming streak due to fresh signs of respite for Hungary's recession-hit economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. job growth rose modestly in January and gains in the prior two months were bigger than initially reported, supporting views the economy's sluggish recovery was on track. [ID:nL1N0B138D\par \par \uc2 "The data, I'd say have a neutral or mildly positive impact," said \uc2 Piotr Bujak\uc2  of \uc2 Nordea\uc2  in Warsaw.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  led gains in emerging Europe until midday after data showing Hungary's seasonally-adjusted Purchasing Managers' Index jumped to 55.9 in January from a revised 49.1 in December.\par \par \uc2 A reading above 50 signals growth in manufacturing and similar surveys showed Polish and Czech manufacturing surveys, which use different methodology than the Hungarian survey, remained stuck below the 50 mark.\par \par \uc2 By 1400 GMT, the zloty was 0.6 percent up at 4.165 per euro and the forint rose 0.4 percent to 291.44, at similar token with the Czech crown to25,599.\par \par \uc2 "The zloty has been underperforming other CEEs over the past few weeks, so now it has more potential to gain amid better attitude to CEE as a whole, even despite improvement in Poland's PMI weaker than in Czech and Hungary."\par \par \uc2 Central Europe's economies are struggling as austerity measures are dampening consumer sentiment and because of slower trade with the crisis-hit euro zone, and central banks are loosening monetary policy, which is weighing on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Romanian leu was 0.3 percent up at 4.365.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said Serbian dinar firmed 0.2 percent to 111.07 due to greater corporate euro sales and government-subsidised lending for small and medium enterprises worth about 120 million euros.\par \par \uc2 CONCERNS REMAIN\par \par \uc2 The forint has lost 3.7 percent since Dec. 1, leading a drop in the region, as it has also been hit by investor concerns that a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, due to take office in March, could use unorthodox monetary tools to boost the economy.\par \par \uc2 Government officials have tried to soothe concerns this week, and Prime Minister Viktor Orban said on Friday that the leadership change needed to be as smooth as possible.\par \par \uc2 CIB analysts said that "considering technical factors as well despite a slowdown in the positive correction in the forint by the end of yesterday, there is a chance that the near two-month weakening trend in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be broken."\par \par \uc2 "Risk sentiment is slightly more supportive, but flows are very low so I would not read much into the current action. I think in February we will remain well-behaved until the (foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) bond is sold," a Budapest-based trader said referring to Hungary's first foreign bond issue since 2011, which could be launched in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in much of the region meet next week, with Poland expected to cut interest rates. In the Czech Republic, rates are already at a "technical zero" and investors will look for clues as to whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will turn to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its next policy tool.\par \par \uc2 Only \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept interest rates steady, held back by inflation concerns that should continue as the country deregulates power and gas prices under pressure from the EU and IMF. The \uc2 Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also meets next week. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2013\par Czech crown                  25,614  25,698  +0,33%    -2,21%\par Polish zloty                 4,167   4,2     +0,79%    -2,23%\par Hungarian forint             291,65  292,7   +0,36%    -0,24%\par Croatian kuna                7,59    7,592   +0,03%    -0,51%\par Romanian leu                 4,363   4,381   +0,41%    +1,83%\par Serbian dinar                111,07  111,2   +0,12%    +1,11%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Reuters bureaus; Writing by Jason Hovet and Radu Marinas; editing by Ron Askew)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EASTEUROPE/ (CEE MARKETS 2) | RBN | PX | BX | HX | ABN | M | RDN | D | E | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130201e921000q8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
