\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Leu bounces off record low, zloty hits 1-yr peak\b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2012\par 17:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dealers cite c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosting leu\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  govt shuffle keeps political tension high\par \par \uc2 * Zloty hits fresh 1-year peak, yields little changed\par \par \uc2 * Crown trails after second straight output decline\par \par \uc2 By Jason Hovet\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Aug 6 (Reuters) - The Romanian leu rebounded 1 percent off a record low on Monday, with dealers saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to give it some respite from a months-long political clash that has paralysed policy and drawn criticism from abroad.\par \par \uc2 Other emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also gained, led by a jump in the Polish zloty to a one-year high due to strong demand for Polish bonds. The Czech crown, however, trailed after data showed industrial output declined for a second straight month.\par \par \uc2 Recession-hit Romania paid a higher price for its debt on Monday. It sold a planned offer of 700 million lei in one-year paper at a 5.66 percent average yield, 37 basis points higher than at a sale in June.\par \par \uc2 Markets were keen to see the results at the tender, Romania's first market foray since a July 29 referendum to impeach suspended President Traian Basescu.\par \par \uc2 After a delay, Romania's Constitutional Court will decide on Aug. 31 whether to validate the referendum, in which a large majority voted to dismiss Basescu, but turnout fell short of a required threshold.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Victor Ponta's government wants his rival Basescu removed, arguing he has overstepped his powers.\par \par \uc2 The power struggle has raised doubts over Romania's \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  standby aid deal that has helped it weather the volatility of the euro zone debt crisis but comes with strict policy requirements.\par \par \uc2 By 1445 GMT, the leu rose 1.4 percent to 4.51 to the euro, well off an all-time low of 4.651 hit on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "I think depreciation bets will soften from now on, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," said a Bucharest trader.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Ponta replaced five ministers in his cabinet after the resignation of an interior minister. Earlier, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mugur Isarescu urged the Balkan country's leaders to reduce the political tension, but added that, given the turmoil, the exchange rate was "almost reasonable".\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't make sense for them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much at this point," a second trader said. "We need to at least know the referendum outcome, otherwise they can only provide short-term support."\par \par \uc2 ZLOTY TESTS 4.0 LEVEL\par \par \uc2 In other trade, Serbia's dinar dipped 0.1 percent after government moves to step up control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted a second resignation at the bank.\par \par \uc2 In the rest of central Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds have gained as investors favour their higher yields compared with negative yields in some safer western European states.\par \par \uc2 Demand pushed Polish five-year bond yields to record lows, boosting the zloty to a one-year peak for a second session. It\par \par \uc2 tested the key 4.0 level, up 0.5 percent since Friday. Yields were up a notch on 5- and 10-year paper.\par \par \uc2 "The market is bullish mainly thanks to the demand for Polish bonds," said a Warsaw-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint gained 0.3 percent while the Czech crown reversed early losses. Stock markets rose for a second day, led by Prague's 1.6 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian three-year bond yields fell below 7 percent for the first time since July 20, down 5 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian assets have rallied since last month, when Budapest got talks with the IMF over a credit deal underway. Analysts expect a financing backstop will help ease borrowing costs for central Europe's most indebted country.\par \par \uc2 The region relies heavily on the euro zone, which is in its third year of crisis, for trade and banking. Data on Monday showed Czech output declined for a second straight month in June, but foreign trade showed a large surplus.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign trade data may be viewed as good news for the Czech crown but development of industrial orders should sour the sentiment," said Radomir Jac, chief analyst for Generali PPF Asset Management. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2012 \par Czech crown                  25.064    25.135  +0.28%   +1.92%\par Polish zloty                 4.041    4.061  +0.49%   +10.49%\par Hungarian forint             276.15    277.03  +0.32%   +13.92%\par Croatian kuna                7.51    7.498   -0.16%   +0.08%\par Romanian leu                 4.57    4.634  +1.4%    -5.45%\par Serbian dinar                118.21    118.06   -0.13%    -9.53%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds           \p