\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  up, but euro worries keep pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2012\par 17:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Big order lifts crown, weakening seen again\par \par \uc2 * Greece, Spanish banks keep investors cautious\par \par \uc2 * Forint nears big 300 level, leu back on path to low\par \par \uc2 By Sam Cage and Jana Mlcochova\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST/PRAGUE, May 18 (Reuters) - Central European emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered from lows on Friday, mirroring the euro's brief recovery, with one Budapest trader saying panic had subsided over the prospects of Greece being forced to leave the euro.\par \par \uc2 The Czech crown, which fell to a fourth-month low this week, recovered the most ground in what one dealer attributed to euro "sell" orders from two big London names. By 1358 GMT the crown\par \par \uc2 was bid up 0.6 percent up at 25.3 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 A Prague-based dealer, however, predicted fresh pressure on the crown. "We could see (EUR/CZK at) 26 again - not today, but in a short time," a Prague-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Czech bonds were steady, supported by expectations of a rate cut as soon as next month after the economy slid deeper in recession in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian bond yields retreated by about 5 basis points from early levels along the curve with Budapest stocks bouncing back to lead regional market gains with 1.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Warsaw and Prague were 0.7 and 0.1 percent up respectively while Romania's fell 1.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Panic over Greece has subsided, expectations are shifting into a more positive direction and Greece may not leave the euro zone," one Budapest-based fixed income trader said.\par \par \uc2 But the trader added that Greece - which holds fresh elections on June 17 - and the euro zone would remain the dominant market-moving factor next week.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint and the Polish zloty have each shed more than 4 percent since a May 6 election left Greek politics in disarray and raised questions about its future in the euro zone, central Europe's most important banking and trading partner.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint was off morning lows, and traded flat on the day, close to the 300 per euro level which it has tested several times since January but failed to break.\par \par \uc2 It briefly hit 299.7 per euro earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 "The level of 300 to the euro provides psychological support, the question is whether it will be able to stop further weakening of the forint, especially if the euro/dollar moves lower again," a Budapest based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Hungary hopes to start aid talks with the International Monetary Fund in June on a loan from the Fund and EU to help avert a potential funding crisis. The country's borrowing costs remain high after years of controversial government policies, including the crisis taxes, which have made investors wary.\par \par \uc2 With the existing windfall tax on telecoms firms already under challenge in European courts, parliament approved on Friday a new charge on phone calls, effective from July 1, to help plug a budget gap in central Europe's most indebted state.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Polish zloty rose 0.5 percent to 4.333. It has found some relief from expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leu was flat on the day at 4.44 per euro, close to an all time low hit earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 "Local politics doesn't help - it feels like 2008 reloaded. The whole world falls apart and Romanian politicians are promising higher wages and pensions," said a Bucharest dealer.\par \par \uc2 In Serbia, the dinar continued to slide a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 50 million euros to stem declines, with investors cautious before a presidential runoff on May 20.\par \par \uc2 The European Bank for Reconstruction and Development said on Friday lacklustre growth in emerging European economies could turn into recession if the euro zone crisis escalates.\par \par \uc2 "The risk that emerging Europe as a whole will re-enter recession within the next 12 months is viewed as high," the EBRD said in a statement accompanying the growth forecasts.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2012 \par Czech crown                  25.302    25.463   +0.64%   +0.96%\par Polish zloty                 4.333     4.356    +0.53%   +3.04%\par Hungarian forint             297.83    297.68   -0.05%   +5.63%\par Croatian kuna                7.553     7.552    -0.01%   -0.49%\par Romanian leu                 4.444     4.442    -0.05%   -2.77%\par  Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds           2-yr T-bond   CZ2YT=RR   +13 basis points to  121bps over bmk*7-yr T-bond   CZ7YT=RR   +4 basis points to  +182bps over bmk*10-yr T-bond   CZ9YT=RR  -5 basis points to  +213bps over bmk*\par Polish treasury bonds           2-yr T-bond   PL2YT=RR   +3 basis points to  +477bps over bmk*5-yr T-bond   PL5YT=RR   +2 basis points to  +454bps over bmk*10-yr T-bond PL10YT=RR   +4 basis points to  +397bps over bmk*\par Hungarian treasury bonds           \par 3-yr T-bond  HU3YT=RR   +3 basis points to  +810bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond  HU5YT=RR   +1 basis points to  +788bps over bmk*