\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Quebec's Export Picture looking brighter\b0\par\par\uc2 1,282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2012\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 9:41 (Dow Jones) Quebec's export growth picture finally looks good after several lackluster years, \uc2 Export Development Canada\uc2  says. Agency expects Quebec's international exports to rise by 7% in 2012 and 2013, reflecting growth in the province's key industrial goods, machinery & equipment and transportation sectors. Among the factors the agency sees driving growth are global demand for commodities and a recovery in the US housing market, as well as strong deliveries for Quebec-based aerospace giant Bombardier (BBD.B.T). (carolyn.m.king@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1338 GMT [Dow Jones] The surge in EUR/RON is down to blown out stop losses, according to Rozalia Pal, chief economist at the Romanian subsidiary of \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . "The collapse of the [Romanian] government was very unexpected. Many banks were short EUR/RON," he says. "If we don't get a new government in the next two weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may allow RON to depreciate to 4.4 against the euro, but it could well reach that level on its own as some London banks heavily sell the leu," he adds. The Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a very limited appetite for RON appreciation. EUR/RON trades at 4.39. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1336 GMT [Dow Jones] Leu losses will be short-term says Raiffeisen but the Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be remain volatile for the next few weeks as the market digests the toppling of the country's government in a no-confidence vote and the potential bringing forward of elections. It's possible that EUR/RON could breach 4.40 but it will only be a short-term development, says Martin Stelzeneder, senior analyst at Raiffeisen Research in Vienna, who expects the cross to return to around 4.35. EUR/RON is at 4.39. (william.kemble-diaz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1332 GMT [Dow Jones] Romanian uncertainty sees RON outflows says Citi adding that the slide in RON seemed to extend after the IMF said it would halt its review of Romania's EUR5 billion aid deal pending the formation of a new government. But Citi's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists aren't too concerned, noting that the country was scheduled to go to the polls in autumn 2012 anyway. "The most likely scenario is that the main opposition parties will put together a new administration in the interim. They support the IMF deal so are unlikely to rock the boat too much. EUR/RON now at 4.393. (jessica.mead@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:31 (Dow Jones) With realized volatility still low, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options that hedge against short-term rate swings are closing the week at new lows. That's led to speculation that options can only move higher as disappointing economic performance in the US and Europe lead to higher volatility. "Markets are obsessed with the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity is keeping volatility low," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  notes. "However, the monetary-transaction mechanism seems to have broken down in EMU, suggesting that the link between \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity and volatility will ease." One options trader notes the spread between short-term EUR/USD options, which cover 1-3 months, and long-term options of a year-plus is at its widest in a year. That's attracting investors to calendar spreads, where short-term options are bid up in exchange for selling longer-dated ones. (matt.walter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1330 GMT [Dow Jones] Spain faces lengthy economic problems when considering the following hefty set of figures, says David Owen, chief European economist at \uc2 Jefferies International\uc2 : as of Q4 2011, Spanish non-financial corporation bank borrowings stood at EUR1.4 trillion, a figure broadly unchanged since 2008. In addition, total outstanding financial liabilities of the general government stood at 86.7% of GDP, not the 68.5% usually quoted, partly because of unpaid bills and trade credits, notes Owen. And now there's Spain's two-notch downgrade to triple-B-plus by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 .(carol.dean@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:28 (Dow Jones) Short-dated Eurodollar futures today project only a modest increase for a key interbank funding rate the next several months even though S&P downgraded Spain and Italian bond yields move higher. Three-month dollar Libor was unchanged in the cash market at 0.46585%, according to the British Bankers Association, and September futures recently priced in a 0.515% rate by Sept. 17's expiration, compared with 0.505% at Thursday's settlement. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2354 or email kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1327 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the unexpected news that the recently installed government has fallen, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "It's a bit of a surprise and we see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come into the rescue as the [EUR/RON] 4.40 level is quite sensitive for them," says Gaelle Blanchard, an emerging-market strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in London. The key question is how this will affect the country's talks with the IMF, Blanchard adds, and warns that the Hungary should be closely watched for ripple effects as well. "It hasn't yet turned into a bigger earthquake yet," she adds. EUR/RON trades at 4.394.(eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1324 GMT [Dow Jones] Who will buy gilts when the BOE stops asks RBC as QE purchases end next week. Strategist Sam Hill notes that in spite of the UK entering technical recession it is unlikely that the MPC will vote for more gilt buybacks at the May meeting. Concludes that "in the medium term the vacuum left by the Bank of England is likely to be filled by pension funds but that they will require long-dated yields to retrace toward 4%." However, in the short-term, continuing risk-aversion could keep safe-haven bonds rich even in the absence of additional QE. The 10-year gilt yields 2.12% and the 30-year yields 3.33%, both 2bps higher on the day. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakness for leu not over says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  because if anything, the fall of the Romanian government adds to regional worries in general, says Peter Kinsella an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the bank. "As a result we would not be surprised to see higher levels [in EUR/RON] trade in the near future," he adds. EUR/RON is just short of the June 2010 high at 4.393. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1317 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish and Italian bonds recovering early losses after a reasonable multi-tranche Italian auction soothes market nerves. In the 10-year space Italy now trades at 5.65%, up 3bps on the day, after having traded above 5.75% earlier, while Spain is currently quoted at 5.86%, a net 5bps higher, after having come close to breaking 6%. Both curves are experiencing slight bear-flattening with yields in the 5-year sector up around 10bps on the session. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1315 GMT [Dow Jones] Romanian leu tumbles but rest of EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady as the Romanian government collapses on a no-confidence motion tabled by the left-leaning opposition. EUR/RON has surged to a June 2010 high but the rest of CEEMEA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unmoved. EUR/RON is at 4.393. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 27, 2012 09:41 ET (13:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120427e84r004pz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
