\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Cold snap undermines Romania's slow recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 11:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * FinMin says q/q contraction possible in Q1\par \par \uc2 * Would be technical recession after Q4 contraction\par \par \uc2 * Maintains full year forecast 1.5-2 pct growth\par \par \uc2 * Govt mulls easing austerity before Nov election\par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst comment, details)\par \par \uc2 By Luiza Ilie\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, March 9 (Reuters) - Romania's economy may suffer its second consecutive quarterly contraction in the first quarter of 2012, dragged into recession by freezing temperatures and heavy snow which hit output, Finance Minister Bogdan Dragoi said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The economy shrank by more than 7 percent in 2009, when Romania was forced to seek a 20 billion euro ($26.5 billion) International Monetary Fund bailout and enforce austerity measures to maintain investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 Its recovery has been slow, with last year's 2.5 percent expansion doing little to make up the lost ground. The effects of the euro zone debt crisis mean the government expects growth of 1.5-2 percent for the whole of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures plunged below minus 20 degrees Celsius in January and February and snow storms blocked roads, railways and ports and even buried many houses in southern Romania, while the army had to rescue stranded travellers.\par \par \uc2 "It is possible we will see technical recession in the first quarter. But the bad weather has not hurt productivity and output, it only blocked the transfer of goods," Dragoi said.\par \par \uc2 "In the first quarter we see ... a marginal (GDP) drop compared with the fourth quarter."\par \par \uc2 There was also a quarterly contraction in the last three months of 2011. The ministry still expects the economy to grow between 1.5 and 2 percent on an annual basis in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 The news had little impact on the leu, which has been the emerging European Union's most stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the euro zone debt crisis has raged, partly due to the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LOOMING ELECTION\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  has completed its IMF bailout and has a new deal in place worth 5 billion euros, to be tapped only if needed.\par \par \uc2 With a parliamentary election due in November, Romania's government - struggling at less than 20 percent support in opinion polls - is examining ways to ease austerity, which included cutting public employees' wages by a quarter and raising sales tax by five percentage points to 24 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Romanian President Traian Basescu said the newly appointed government should consider ways to restore public sector pay to pre-austerity levels by June, a move Dragoi said would add 2 billion lei ($609 million) for the 2012 budget.\par \par \uc2 Cutting social security contributions by five percentage points, another possibility to improve living standards in a country where some villages and even city districts have no running water or sewerage, would have a budgetary impact of about 2.3 billion lei, Dragoi added.\par \par \uc2 He said the government will stick to its deficit target of 1.9 percent of gross domestic product for this year.\par \par \uc2 The two measures together would amount to some 0.7 percent of GDP. The IMF has said there is not enough money to increase salaries and pensions, for now at least, but has previously given Romania some leeway because of its generally strong commitment, after cutting the budget gap to 4.4 percent of GDP last year from 6.5 in 2010.\par \par \uc2 "The markets may penalise the government if they decide to raise wages or pensions or cut social security contributions," said Ionut Dumitru, chief economist at Raiffeisen Bank Romania.\par \par \uc2 "Taking such measures at a time when the economy is performing worse than expected may be seen as a slippage. They need to be very careful about what they decide." ($1 = 3.2823 Romanian lei) ($1 = 0.7534 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 ROMANIA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | M | RDN | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120309e839000nn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
