\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Forint up after CPI but euro zone crisis weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,068 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2011\par 15:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates price movements)\par \par \uc2 * Bonds show no reaction to higher CPI data\par \par \uc2 * Crown off multi-month low, zloty weak in holiday trade\par \par \uc2 * GDP data next week expected to show impact of euro crisis\par \par \uc2 By Jason Hovet and Sam Cage\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE/BUCHAREST, Nov 11 (Reuters) - The Hungarian forint firmed on Friday after higher-than-expected inflation data, but the widening euro zone debt crisis continued to weigh on other emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The forint and the crown have struggled in the past week as the euro zone crisis spiralled into a new, more dangerous phase with attention now turned to Italy's debt mountain.\par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product data, due across the region next week, will indicate how much the euro zone's problems are slowing growth in emerging Europe, whose economies are dominated by exports to their western neighbours.\par \par \uc2 Higher inflation in Hungary fuelled the case for the bank to raise interest rates, a dealer said, helping the forint\par \par \uc2 rise 0.6 percent to 310.75 to the euro. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed within sight of its all-time low of 317.45 hit in March 2009, a level it has been approaching this week.\par \par \uc2 The government has said it will wait for Germany to officially change its economic outlook before revising its own growth projection of 1.5 pct for 2012, which most analysts say is too optimistic.\par \par \uc2 "Of course the market will be interested in the GDP figure, if it is too bad relative to the consensus, people will ask why the government waits to change its forecast," a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian inflation ticked up to 3.9 percent in October, higher than analysts expected. With the forint inching towards a new low, analysts have said emergency rate hikes, like the 300 basis point rise implemented at the start of the 2008 financial crisis, cannot be ruled out.\par \par \uc2 "We see a clear risk of an emergency hike to stem HUF weakening. In our baseline scenario, however, we have unchanged rates until a rate cut in H2 2012," said Bjarke Roed-Frederiksen of Nordea Markets.\par \par \uc2 Debt markets, however, showed little reaction to the inflation data, with Hungarian yields falling by up to 11 basis points from highs hit on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "Sentiment is slightly better although there is a lot of news knocking about," a bond trader said. "Nobody cares about inflation right now, because growth is so subdued and fears over growth are so strong that there are no real concerns about inflation."\par \par \uc2 By 1400 GMT, the zloty was down 0.5 percent at 4.424 to the euro with local markets closed for a national holiday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the zloty in September and October.\par \par \uc2 Dealers have also suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at times. The leu was up 0.1 percent at 4.347 per euro.\par \par \uc2 CZECH RATES\par \par \uc2 The crown was down 0.3 percent at 25.7 per euro and set for its biggest weekly loss in two years after outperforming this year the forint and the Polish zloty, which have both shed more than 10 percent since Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Poland or Hungary, the Czechs are facing little inflationary pressure, leaving space for rate cuts in the face of a weaker economic outlook as trade with the euro zone slows.\par \par \uc2 The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was near its lowest level since June 2010 after losing 2.5 percent in the past week. Prior to this week's weakening, the crown, seen as the region's safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in central Europe still trading above its level at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Dealers say London hedge funds have been behind the crown's weakening this week. In the past two weeks, the Finance Ministry, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and European Commission have sharply cut their Czech growth outlook for next year to 0.7-1.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vice-governor Vladimir Tomsik was the lone voter in favour of a 25 basis point interest rate reduction at the board's Nov. 3 meeting. The board left the bank's base rate unchanged at a record low 0.75 percent, minutes showed.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting, the bank's governing board agreed that risks and uncertainties to the outlook were extremely high, mainly due to the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "This morning's minutes from the MPC's Nov. 3 rate meeting kept to a dovish tone, but ultimately said all policy options were on the table until further developments are seen in the euro area, which could offer the unit a bit of support," 4Cast said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, in this uncertain environment, relief is only seen to be temporary and EUR/CZK downside is seen to be limited." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2010 \par Czech crow