\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-Romania annual inflation slows to 3.5 pct in Sept\b0\par\par\uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2011\par 09:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, Oct 11 (Reuters) - Romania's inflation fell sharply to 3.5 percent in September from August's 4.3 percent, beating market expectations and entering the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target band, indicating it may have room to start cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say slowing inflation, helped by steep falls in food prices, coupled with lower global growth forecasts may mean rates could be cut from the current 6.25 percent, although risks for inflation remained strong in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which targets inflation at 2-4 percent this year and next, has kept rates flat at a record low since May 2010, but still the highest among emerging EU states.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices were down 0.2 percent on the month with food prices falling some 1.2 percent. Non-food prices rose 0.2 percent while services were up 0.6 percent, the National Statistics Board said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION\par \par \uc2 The Romanian leu was a touch weaker against the euro, trading at 4.323 at 0730 GMT, compared with levels around 3.3190 seen before the data release.\par \par \uc2 MARKET FORECAST\par \par \uc2 A median forecast of 14 analysts polled by Reuters earlier this month showed Romanian inflation at 3.9 percent year-on-year in September. Monthly forecast was 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 KEY FIGURES\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ============================================================\par  CPI (pct change)             SEPTEMBER       AUGUST\par  yr/yr                        3.5               4.3\par  mth/mth                     -0.2              -0.4\par  ============================================================\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANALYST COMMENTS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DEALER WITH FOREIGN BANK IN BUCHAREST\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The leu is a touch weaker because, at least in theory, a sharp drop in inflation leads to expectations of rate cuts."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But in practice, I think the market would like a 4.4 level to the euro gradually. And so the leu weakens slightly without \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I say without \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic recovery is export driven."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NICOLAIE ALEXANDRU-CHIDESCIUC, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT ING IN BUCHAREST\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is good news but a closer look at the details shows the drop stems from volatile elements."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation will continue to fall in the coming months and it is possible to see rates lower than 2 percent in March-April 2012."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But this disinflationary trend is not sustainable and it would likely be easily reversed even if next year records a normal harvest, not a poor one."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will see inflation rise in June-July."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What's debatable is how high inflation can go. For instance, for the end of 2012, we have a forecast close to 4 percent."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even before the data release we were expecting an interest rate cut from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February 2012."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For now this pronounced drop in inflation is purely temporary. It couldn't be seen as a trend that will consolidate in the medium term. There are still significant risks to inflation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In theory, a drop so pronounced in inflation would lead to lower longer-dated money market rates. It is also possible that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be a little more relaxed about a weaker leu \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , given the slowdown in exports, but nothing excessive."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CATALINA MOLNAR, SENIOR ECONOMIST, RBS ROMANIA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation falling to 3.5 percent is below our expectations as the favourable effect from a drop in vegetables and fruits prices was stronger than we anticipated."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The effect of a good crop was expected to have an impact in the autumn months, but not at this magnitude. Considering this, our end of the year inflation forecast is now down to 3.5 percent and considering it includes an aggressive scenario of hikes in heating prices, we might even see lower a year-end inflation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We must be aware that the favorable impact from the food sector on inflation from this year could come back as a boomerang next year as inflation could rise again to above 4 percent in the summer and autumn period due to an unfavorable base effect."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Considering all these, the maneuvering room the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has to cut rates is rising and we might even see a rate cut to 6 percent at the last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting from this year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Next year, considering a medium inflation forecast of 3.5 percent and a persistent negative output gap, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could continue cutting rates to 5.5 percent."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DAN BUCSA, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT UNICREDIT TIRIAC BANK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation surprised to the downside for the fourth month in a row. Volatile prices fell further on very good crops. It is surprising that sharp RON depreciation during September had very limited effects on inflation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The new inflation reading means that the inflation target will probably be met (3.7 percent our corrected forecast for end 2011)."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Annual core 2 inflation fell to 3 percent and core 3 (excluding tobacco and alcohol) went down to 2.7 percent in September, increasing the probability of rate cuts."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Despite the very good inflation numbers, we don't expect a rate cut in November, unless there is comprehensive action from Euro Zone leaders to quench risk aversion."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We maintain our call for the first cut in early 2012, with the possibility of a hastier cutting cycle especially if economic growth is weak in the first half of 2012." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  BACKGROUND     \tab \par  LINKS:     \tab \par - For LIVE Romanian economic data releases, click on   \par \tab \par - Overview of Romanian macroeconomic indicators        \par \tab \par - Key data releases in central Europe   \par \tab \par - For Romanian speed debt speed guide                 \par \tab \par - For Romanian money markets data click on            \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Ioana Patran and Luiza Ilie; Editing by Sam Cage)\f28 \par \par \uc2 ROMANIA-CPI/ (INSTANT VIEW 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111014e7ab000h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
