\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Slight return of risk appetite lifts CEE assets\b0\par\par\uc2 1,207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2011\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds bonds, updated stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Forint leads \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  firming; CEE stock indices up 3.5-4 pct\par \par \uc2 * Traders eye possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  cbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Govt bond yields slump, making curves less steep\par \par \uc2 * Euro crisis can keep regional markets volatile\par \par \uc2 By Sam Cage and Sandor Peto\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST/BUDAPEST, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The zloty and forint led emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher on Tuesday, benefiting from the return of some appetite for riskier assets and underpinned by the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets tracked a rebound in Asian shares from multi-month lows on hopes euro zone officials will act to corral Greece's debt woes -- though traders and analysts said any relief from recent losses may be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian government bond yields declined by 18-38 basis points from multi-month highs, with long-term yields falling more. Poland's bond yield curve also got less steep as long-term yields fell by about 10 basis points.\par \par \uc2 "I think this is a correction and high volatility will stay," one Budapest-based fixed income trader said. "I don't think that there is a final resolution to the European debt crisis. What we can see is a ray of hope."\par \par \uc2 The euro zone is central Europe's key trading partner, making the region vulnerable to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's debt crisis even though debt levels in central Europe are relatively low.\par \par \uc2 By 1342 GMT the zloty was 0.8 percent higher at 4.381 per euro, moving further away from the 27-month low of 4.537 that prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the state to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint gained 1.3 percent to 285.95 against the euro, and one Budapest-based dealer noted some flows which also pushed down the zloty against the forint to support levels at 65.20-30.\par \par \uc2 The zloty has fallen 9 percent since mid-year and the forint about 7 percent. The region's safe-haven unit, the Czech crown\par \par \uc2 , shed less than 1 percent and the leu -- defended by the Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- eased 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday the crown gained 0.7 and the leu 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last seen in the market on Sept. 22.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vehicles were on the offer towards 4.3100 the other days, but I am not very sure it was an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  per se," said a Bucharest dealer. "I think they would step in around 4.3200 more aggressively and if not there, 4.3500."\par \par \uc2 The region's main national equity indices rose by 3.5-4 percent after deep losses in the past weeks.\par \par \uc2 ZLOTY, FORINT RISKS\par \par \uc2 In times of risk aversion investors sold the forint and the zloty almost indiscriminately and differentiated between the two units only occasionally.\par \par \uc2 A rebound of the zloty would cut state debt, reducing the likelihood that the Polish government will need painful fiscal measures to keep the debt below constitutional limits.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in the debt-to-GDP ratio assumed in the debt strategy is surely positive for bond prices, but one has to keep in mind that elections are key now and it will be the new government that will decide on the budget," said Bartlomiej Wit, the head of fixed income trading at \uc2 ING Bank Slaski\uc2  in Warsaw.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about public finances have been a key factor weakening the zloty. Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski budget plans would stay on track even if growth slows to 3 percent next year and there was no risk of public debt exceeding the key threshold despite the recent weakening of the zloty.\par \par \uc2 The government on Tuesday approved a budget that envisages economic growth of 4 percent and average annual inflation of 2.8 percent, but final approval by parliament hinges on the outcome of an election set for Oct. 9.\par \par \uc2 Rostowski said the debt strategy assumed an average zloty rate at 4.35 per euro.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian assets also face risks as the government has just launched a scheme to allow households to repay foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans at discount exchange rates, forcing commercial banks to swallow big losses.\par \par \uc2 "My feeling is that this issue will get more attention when queues line up at banks and people start to think that it will be more than 10-15 percent of borrowers who want to repay debt," the Budapest bond trader said.\par \par \uc2 -----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------- \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2011 \par Czech crown        24.43    24.611  +0.74%   +2.33%\par Polish zloty       4.381    4.418  +0.84%    -9.66%\par Hunga