\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Romania Aug inflation quickens, just below fcast\b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 09:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Heightens cbank dilemma of balancing inflation, recovery\par \par \uc2 * Most analysts see interest rates unchanged till end 2010\par \par \uc2 * Leu shows little reaction to data\par \par \uc2 (Adds more details, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , comment)\par \par \uc2 By Sam Cage and Luiza Ilie\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, Sept 10 (Reuters) - Romania's annual inflation quickened in August due to a hike in value added tax, heightening the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problems with controlling prices while trying to give the depressed economy a helping hand.\par \par \uc2 Prices rose 7.6 percent from a year ago, just below analysts' average forecast but up from 7.1 percent in July, when the VAT increase was first implemented. Inflation was 0.2 percent on the month <ROCPI=ECI>.\par \par \uc2 Bucharest is struggling to bring its fiscal deficit down to keep a 20 billion euro International Monetary Fund-led bailout deal on track and maintain investor confidence, and it chose to hike VAT by 5 percentage points to 24 percent to boost revenues.\par \par \uc2 "Given the upward path of inflation we feel that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave interest rates unchanged," said Melania Hancila, chief economist at Volksbank in Bucharest.\par \par \uc2 "Although a deepening of the economic crisis created the premises for monetary policy easing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced to hold fire due to increased risks and high inflation."\par \par \uc2 A median forecast from analysts polled by Reuters earlier this month put inflation at 7.7 percent on the year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which targets inflation at 2.5-4.5 percent in 2010, sees end-year price growth at 7.8 percent. <RO/POLL1>\par \par \uc2 While the economy nudged out of recession in the second quarter, the government, IMF and analysts all expect gross domestic product to shrink in the second half as the higher VAT and wage cuts hit spending.\par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep interest rates unchanged until the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The National Statistics Board said costs for food items rose 0.4 percent on the month in August. Non-food costs were flat, while services rose 0.4 percent. Inflation was 0.2 percent on the month.\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu was little changed after the data and traded at 4.272 per euro by 0822 GMT, 0.1 percent higher on the day <EURRON=>.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is driven mainly by political risk, as the fragile coalition government struggles to defend its austerity measures and grip on power, and large swings are expected to be fought with covert \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ROMANIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -INFLATION/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RDN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100913e69a000gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
