\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Romania \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected - ING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2010\par 09:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to protect the leu around EUR/RON4.4, says ING Bank. "This does not mean that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wants a stronger RON, but it would mean EUR/RON is capped to the upside," the bank says. Adds, if there is a favourable outcome from the IMF on Friday, then the RON could strengthen versus the euro. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 1.3% at 17,470.77, tracking weak Asian markets as soft data from U.S., China reinforces concerns about fragile global economic recovery. Index breadth negative as 27 of 30 stocks lower with decline led by metals, financials and technology stocks; heavyweights drag. "The culprit remains shaky global markets. Volatility has jumped amid renewed concerns over weak economic numbers coming out of China and the U.S.," says brokerage \uc2 India Infoline\uc2 . Tips support at 17,460. \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY) down 3.3% at INR164.20, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 2.6% at INR839.40, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) down 1.6% at INR1,069.80, \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2  (532540.BY) down 2.4% at INR732.95. S&P CNX Nifty down 1.4% at 5236.75. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] Still sell EUR/USD on any rally, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . The bank notes a positive 6-day ECB tender later in the day should "offer the EUR a cushion." But, the bank adds, "a full break of the 1.2300-1.2350 area is needed for a more sustained recovery." The pair is now at 1.2211. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0730 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts open higher Thursday, supported by further weakness in equities. Concerns over the pace of the global economic recovery is dragging on risky assets, boosting the appeal of core fixed income securities. BOE's Miles Thursday joined MPC member Posen in arguing against a rate hike currently despite high inflation, saying the UK remained vulnerable to adverse developments in the euro zone. On the data front, the CIPS manufacturing PMI is out 0830 GMT, while the DMO auctions GBP800M UKT 2047 linker. September gilt contract is up 0.17 at 121.22, and the 10-year gilt is up at 111.769 to yield 3.317%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] The combination of general pressure on the euro and the SNB calling a halt to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has opened the downside in EUR/CHF, says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . Switzerland's favorable relative growth and interest rate outlook, combined with a high probability of the unwinding of CHF loans, will further support the franc in coming month, says the bank. Revises its 3-, 6- and 12-month EUR/CHF forecasts to 1.30, 1.28 and 1.35, from 1.37, 1.37 and 1.41 respectively. Danske does slot in a caveat however, saying increased speculative interest in the franc and selling EUR/CHF as a hedge against a euro collapse means the latest tumble in the cross could be overdone, and any improvement in euro sentiment could see a sharp upward correction in EUR/CHF, which now trades at 1.3130. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end slightly lower, on profit-taking after recent four consecutive sessions of gains. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index ends at 125.63, off record high of 125.70, expected to consolidate in 125.60-125.70 tomorrow; index yesterday ended at 125.66. "The bond market will likely keep consolidating with upward bias. We expect China to keep the benchmark interest rate unchanged until the second quarter of 2011," Sealand Securities says in note. Two purchasing managers' surveys show China's manufacturing activity expanded at slower pace in June, making it less likely that Beijing will introduce more tightening measures. 20-year bond issued in 2001 ends at CNY108.44 vs yesterday's close of CNY108.45, yield at 3.3422% vs 3.3412%. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] Aussie banks are facing ever growing funding costs, a dynamic that could cause lenders to hike their mortgage rates and do much work for RBA in its battle to damp demand. Although most expect policy to stay on hold next week, thereafter risk growing banks of all sizes will move outside RBA, thus keeping official cash on hold for longer than most expect. "You don't have to be Einstein to work out there's going to be significant margin pressure on banks to continue to absorb elevated costs," says Simon Lewis, executive head of funding at regional lender Suncorp. See =FOCUS: Australia Bank Funding Costs May Torpedo RBA Tightening. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] A combination of the strong Tankan survey and higher aversion to risk has helped to drive the USD/JPY down to "attractive buying levels" around Y88, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . The bank argues this is a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level given the Japanese government has made it clear that levels under 90 are unjustified. "Long positions in USD/JPY therefore offer comparatively little potential for losses while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is expected to appreciate notably in case of returning risk appetite," the bank says. The pair is now at Y88.45. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Stoxx 50 -1.8% at 2527.20, opening up on the back foot Thursday, tracking falls overnight on Wall Street and in Asia. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warning that it is reviewing Spain's sovereign credit rating rattles sentiment and raises further doubts over Europe's economic recovery. Meanwhile, data from China shows manufacturing activity slowed in June, exacerbating investors' fears about the strength of the global economic recovery, adds SVS Securities. Looking ahead, Thursday's session will see the Spanish Treasury's auction of EUR2.5Bln to EUR3.5Bln of five-year bonds, the ECB's tender of unlimited six-day funds, manufacturing PMIs in Europe at 0800 GMT and the UK at 0830 GMT, and the manufacturing ISM in the U.S. at 1400 GMT. (ishaq.siddiqi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] Overnight \uc2 Moody's\uc2  damped the market's renewed interest in buying euros by putting Spain's Aaa rating on review for a possible downgrade. Coupled with a weak ADP jobs report and a sliding China manufacturing PMI and risk appetite is definitely on the wane ahead of Friday's payroll lottery. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , EUR/CHF plummeted to another all-time low of 1.3073, while USD/JPY printed a near 2-month low of 88.08 and sterling lost its shine after yet another MPC member, David Miles, ganged up on interest-rate hawk Sentance, saying there was no need for a rate hike yet. EUR/USD is just about hanging onto the 1.22 handle, however, if equities take a tumble or peripheral bond spreads start to widen then the recent 1.2150 low could well come under attack. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0717 GMT [Dow Jones] Initial losses in European bourses, negative U.S. stock futures suggest Singapore shares expected to remain depressed for rest of session. STI off 0.5% at 2820.24, may close above 2800. "The intermediate STI outlook is weak and, from a technical standpoint, looking rather bearish, reflecting what we believe is a lack of confidence in the G3 (economies') recovery at this point in time," says Phillip Securities; "in the short to medium term, another leg down would not surprise us." Market breadth at 3 decliners for each gainer. Among STI components, cyclical and high-beta stocks such as NOL (N03.SG), Olam (O32.SG), \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) continue to lead declines, but yield plays up as investors flock to defensive stocks, including \uc2 Singapore Press Holdings\uc2  (T39.SG), Starhub (CC3.SG), SMRT (S53.SG). (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS fall on weaker June PMI data, also on disappointing U.S. job data. 1-year down 2 bps at 2.23%-2.30%, 5-year down 2 bps at 2.98%-3.06%. Shanghai-based foreign bank trader says short-term swaps have limited downside room as current levels have priced in expectations liquidity conditions will ease after Agricultural Bank of China ends its subscription. Adds, investors closely watching whether 5-year will break strong support at 3.0%. China official CFLP PMI falls to 52.1 in June from 53.9 in May, while \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI falls to 50.4 in June from 52.7 in May. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) [ 07-01-10 0335ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100701e6710005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
