\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Russia devalues rouble again, Romania leu down\b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2009\par 13:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Russia allows rouble to weaken for 3rd time in 4 days\par \par \uc2 * Romania leu falls despite recovery from historic lows\par \par \uc2 * Ukraine-Russia gas row enters second week\par \par \uc2 By Sebastian Tong\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 14 (Reuters) - Russia on Wednesday allowed its rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yet again as emerging assets struggled to extend gains beyond the Asian session.\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu slipped after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide to historic lows while a gas dispute between Russia and Ukraine that has squeezed supply to Europe entered its second week with few signs of easing.\par \par \uc2 Emerging European shares were mostly weak but the benchmark MSCI Emerging Markets Index <.MSCIEF> was up for the first time in six successive sessions, reflecting strong Asian gains, to inch 0.68 percent higher to 554.64 by 1120 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Emerging sovereign debt spreads <11EMJ> were 2 basis points tighter to trade at 668 bps over U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 Worries over the global economy continued to dominate sentiment -- Wednesday data showed euro zone industrial production falling for the seventh straight month -- with some emerging market governments to revise 2009 economic targets.\par \par \uc2 Turkey, which remains in talks with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for a loan, has cut its 2009 growth target to below 1 percent from 4 percent, according to a newspaper report. [ID:nLE115983]\par \par \uc2 Hungary's prime minister said the country's economy would shrink much more in 2009 than earlier thought and force the government to redraw its economic plans. [ID:nLE315552]\par \par \uc2 "There's uncertainty about the world economy, how much of a fiscal stimulus would come from the U.S. with the new president taking office, whether the lowering of interest rates by central banks around the world will succeed in stimulating the world economy," said Nigel Rendell, emerging markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "People also realise there is going to be less money going into emerging markets this year because major economies will be issuing a lot of bonds."\par \par \uc2 EMERGING \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Russia, which has been among the biggest casualties of an emerging markets selldown, posted figures showing a record net capital outflow of $129.9 billion last year, a third more than forecast. [ID:nLD448601]\par \par \uc2 Russia's finance ministry has said that its 2009 budget could be recalculated to take into account the impact of the fall in oil prices. Moscow staged the third mini-devaluation of the rouble in four days, allowing it to fall 1.4 percent against its euro-dollar basket <RUS=MCX> from the previous session to trade at 36.29.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the unit is set to fall further to reflect weakening oil prices and an economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to at least 38.00 versus the euro-basket," said \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in a client note.\par \par \uc2 The sparring between Russia and its smaller neighbour Ukraine over gas has done little to improve the risk perception of the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 Russian gas flow to Europe remains disrupted with Moscow accusing Kiev of deliberately siphoning supply meant for European customers. Russia turned off supplies to Ukraine on Jan. 1 over a contract dispute. [ID:nLD73352]\par \par \uc2 The cost of insuring Russian sovereign debt rose, with the country's five-year credit default swaps quoted at 680-690 bps, 6 bps higher than previous close.\par \par \uc2 Most emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weaker, with the Czech crown among the day's biggest losers.\par \par \uc2 The crown fell some 1.5 percent against the euro <EURCZK=>, hit by Wednesday's figures for November showing the largest fall in the country's industrial output since 2001.\par \par \uc2 Falling euro zone demand has added weight to calls for swift and bold interest rate cuts in the country. [ID:nLE296066]\par \par \uc2 "The Czech automobile industry, which is of huge importance for the total economy, is suffering seriously from the global crisis in the automobile sector ... The automobile industry also has huge importance for the Slovak, Polish and Hungarian economies. We therefore also expect very disappointing industrial production numbers for Poland due to be released next week," said \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  in a report.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday helped \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leu to recover after it hit a historic low of 4.3530 against the euro on Tuesday but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained weak.\par \par \uc2 The leu <EURRON=> pared earlier losses against the euro to trade 0.3 percent down against the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is expected to test record lows in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 "Government funding capacity is deteriorating rapidly," noted \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Kylie MacLellan; editing by Stephen Nisbet)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090117e51e000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
