\par\par \b \uc2 EURONOMICS: Euro-Zone Bailouts Seen Hurting Emerging Econs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Emsden   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2008\par 18:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MILAN (Dow Jones)--Governments and central banks have begun to bring relief to the credit crisis in Western Europe, but they may have transferred the burden to economies on the European periphery.\par \par \uc2 "The crisis in Western Europe and the U.S. is easing, but there's no easing in Eastern Europe, where it looks like the credit crunch is only intensifying," said Lars Christensen, chief analyst at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 Weaker Western banks may have been saved by government rescue packages, but now their incentive is to channel resources towards buying back the new shares issued to receive that temporary assistance.\par \par \uc2 "The effect is to transfer the credit crunch from the banks to the corporates and also emerging-market economies," said Simon Smollett, an analyst at Calyon in London. "Emerging-market economies will find their money markets will tighten and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  still continue to slide, because the demand from Western banks is a return of capital."\par \par \uc2 Romania, Bulgaria, Croatia and Turkey appear at risk of joining Hungary and Ukraine as economies in line for international assistance. None of these countries has a sovereign credit rating strong enough to allow credible bank rescue plans along the lines of those launched by euro-zone governments.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they're proceeding in a piecemeal fashion. This week, Serbia raised deposit insurance to avoid a run on its banks, Kazakhstan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a $5 billion fund to support local banks, and Bulgaria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased reserve requirements on lenders under its purview.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tried to broker interbank lending in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mitigate a liquidity squeeze. Its government is now offering a tax amnesty for Turks wishing to repatriate savings to the country.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Friday. That helped foil an "attack against the leu," Governor Mugur Isarescu told local press Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The battle rages on, though, as interest rates on Romania's money market remain far above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 10.25% policy rate, already the highest in the 27-member E.U. Isarescu is pleading with banks not to use the local Robor interbank lending rate as price guidance for loans, saying rates of around 18% are skewed by market turmoil. But banks are unlikely to comply, analysts say, given that overnight rates have spiked to 60% and above.\par \par \uc2 That spike has helped support the leu but "will crush the local banking system and then leave them even more exposed in a few weeks' time," said Calyon's Smollett.\par \par \uc2 That was not the goal of the Western bank rescues. Moreover, if Eastern European economies begin to flail and cut back sharply on imports, the euro-zone slowdown will be exacerbated.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Eastern European countries with large external deficits - especially those highly exposed to short-term or hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt - need to boost their savings after years of rapid, credit-fired growth.\par \par \uc2 "You can't avoid the hangover," said \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 's Christensen. The usual ways to do that are tightening fiscal policy or hiking interest rates. "There will be an economic slump, but you kind of want a slump because these economies have been growing too fast," he said.\par \par \uc2 Outside institutions such as the International Monetary Fund or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be able to offer help, but such assistance comes at a price.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine this week announced plans to force down its budget deficit, which almost certainly points to a cutback in the capital spending aimed at boosting long-term growth. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also signaling that it will let the hryvnia, which usually tracks the U.S. dollar, weaken.\par \par \uc2 Such moves probably amount to gestures of reciprocity for expected help from the International Monetary Fund, said Alexander Pecherstyn, an economist for ING in Kiev.\par \par \uc2 In the same vein, Hungary announced a tougher fiscal stance the same day as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unprecedented offer of liquidity to unclog that country's frozen money markets.\par \par \uc2 Pressure for the ECB to do the same for neighbors may grow if emerging-market funding pressures persist, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. But Goldman argues that's an unlikely outcome for outliers such as Romania and Bulgaria, and all but off the table for countries outside the European Union.\par \par \uc2 -By Christopher Emsden, Dow Jones Newswires; +39-02-58-21-99-05; chris.emsden@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-10-08 1602GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1110   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI000002008102