\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Holiday Prompts Flat Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 1,431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2008\par 06:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0700 Czech April CPI \par           +6.6%YY vs +7.1%YY \par  \par 0900/0700 Czech March industrial output \par           +5.5%YY vs +11.3%YY \par  \par 0900/0700 Czech April jobless \par           5.2%(6)      5.6% \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.3895-55  3.3923-43  -0.08  3.3983  3.3861   -5.86 \par EUR/HUF 251.90-35  251.90-20   0.00  251.90  252.35   -0.38 \par EUR/CZK 25.040-70  25.045-75  -0.02  25.059  25.050   -5.50 \par EUR/SKK 31.920-60  31.935-75  -0.05  31.940  31.953   -4.97 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: The euro is expected to break the PLN3.48 barrier Monday, "although this will be more difficult than last month's PLN3.50 - PLN3.40 range, as there are far more constraints," a local trader said. "Friday's performance has opened a window for more transactions," he added. "However, there are several unfavorable factors outside of the region, such as the core market's performance," he said. Important data is scarce next week, with investors waiting for production figures the week after.\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty appreciated Friday, as the euro broke under the PLN3.40 level due to weak levels on high yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Turkish lira, the trader said.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was trading at PLN3.3906, down from PLN3.4141, late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: The forint is expected to be "volatile and directionless" Monday, said a local trader. "There is quite strong (euro) support for a HUF251 figure, but we shall have to see," he added.\par \par \uc2 The forint strengthened Friday, "although not due to any important market movers," the trader said. "It's simply reacting to global markets and the performance of the region," he continued.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was trading at HUF251.96, down from HUF252.73 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: The koruna is expected to strengthen a little Monday, "although there is potential for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken, which we are concerned about," a strategist said. "Inflation data due next week is expected to report on a continued downward trend, and if this is so, it may reduce the chances of rate hikes, currently estimated to be between 25 - 50 basis points. If this happens the koruna may weaken," he said.\par \par \uc2 The koruna was almost flat Friday, "because of nothing in particular," the strategist said. "Just global and regional market tracking, and technical trading had an influence," he added.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was quoted at CZK25.075, up from CZK25.008 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: Polish bond yields are expected to remain flat Monday, as there is no important data due, a local trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish bond yields were flat Friday, despite the zloty's gains as trades were scarce because of investors waiting for next week's data on inflation and industrial production, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the average yield on the 2-year paper was at 6.21%, compared with 6.20% late Tuesday, whilst the 5-year was at 6.14%, compared with 6.14%, and the 10-year was at 5.99%, compared with 5.99%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: Hungarian bond yields are expected to strengthen a little Tuesday (Monday is a public holiday), "purely as a reaction to Thursday's large weakening of 15 basis points across the curve," a local trader said. "Hopefully we'll see a bit of action on the market," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bond yields were almost unchanged Friday. "More or less nothing happened, and I think this was because of the three-day holiday we've just had," the trader said. "It was the quietest of days," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the three-year paper was yielding 9.29%, compared with 9.29% late Tuesday, the five-year was yielding 8.95%, compared with 8.95%, and the 10-year was yielding 8.14%, compared with 8.16%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: The April inflation data release will be the key driver for Czech Treasury bond trades Monday. As analysts expect the consumer price index to continue easing from its record high, the data reading will reinforce expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will keep its monetary policy unchanged for the next few months, one Prague trader said. This in turn may help solidify yields, the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's market activity was very calm as many traders were away from their desks, having bridged Thursday's bank holiday with the weekend. Late Friday, the yield on the benchmark 10-year paper with a 4.% coupon, maturing in 2017, was at 4.62%, down from 4.65% late Wednesday. Local markets were closed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   STOCKS \par  \par WIG20          2998.59   loss  7.6  dn 0.3% \par BUX           23119.46   loss  18.8 dn 1.1% \par PX             1636.8    loss  N/A  dn 1.6% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15  3300.89 loss 1.61 dn 0.005% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The WIG20 shed 0.3% to end the day at 2998.59 on end-session selling. The psychological resistance level at 3000 held for most of the day and triggered in-session selling, a local broker said. KGHM ended down 3.5% as better-than-expected 1Q earnings triggered profit taking. Lotos lost 2.4%. Bank stocks rebounded from Thursday losses; BZ WBK gained 1.7%; Pekao rose 1%; PKO BP was up by 0.3%. TPSA was seen as defensive play, finishing up 0.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: The BUX dropped 0.1% to end the day at 23119.46 amid pessimistic sentiment, a Questor Securities broker said. A trader regards Friday's decline as timely after recent gains. "Investors have become more cautious after the U.S. economic data and Trichet's comments," he said. Most activity took place during the very first hour of trade and then a holiday mood set in ahead of a three-day weekend. Richter fell 3% after 1Q results, \f28 \uc2 \f2 OTP Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 0.7% and Mangyar Telekom rose 0.5%. MOL gained 1.4% on no apparent company news. A Trader expects prices to climb slightly when the market reopens Tuesday, unless Monday's U.S. earnings reveal more skeletons in the closet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: The PX fell 1.6% to end the day at 1636.8, led by Erste Bank and CEZ, closing down 3.4% and 1.6% respectively, largely on profit-taking after gains earlier this week. Traders said that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2 , closing down 0.9%, and CEZ, which are both due to release their 1Q results next week, will be the key trading drivers Monday, helping trading volumes to keep recovering from recent lows. Among other actively-traded stocks: NWR, the London and Prague traded coal stock, ended down 0.2% in Prague at CZK455.9 on profit-taking after its successful listing at GBP13.25/share, completed Tuesday and after its bankers successfully complete their green-shoe option in the company. [ 05-12-08 0016ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Global financial market instability remains an obstacle in Poland's drive to adopt the euro, Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Slawomir Skrzypek said late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: French auto components multinational \f28 \uc2 \f2 Faurecia SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (12114.FR) will open a EUR20 million plant producing exhausts in the eastern Czech town of Karvina as part of a wider shake-up of its Czech business, the domestic CTK news agency reported Sunday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: Leftist Prime Minister Robert Fico warned against a resurgence of the right-wing Fidesz party in Hungary on Saturday in a blunt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the neighboring country's affairs.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ROMANIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The government says a train has derailed in the country, killing one person and injuring five people. Railway police say two and a half carriages of the train came off the tracks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Prime Minister Sergey Stanishev promised Sunday tighter controls on the use of EU funds, after a portion was recently frozen by Brussels due to corruption concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: As EU newcomer Bulgaria aims to attract ever larger flows of foreign investment, authorities and analysts have raised fears that part of the money streaming into the country may have been laundered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SERBIA: Pro-Western forces in Serbia on Monday claimed victory after general elections that gave them a strong mandate to move closer to the European Union despite tensions over the independence of Kosovo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Gary Adams, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9478; gary.adams@dowjones.com [ 05-12-08 0016ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020080512e45c0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
