\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Romania's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Chief's Job Is Getting Harder   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Emsden   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2008\par 18:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Spare a thought for Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Mugur Isarescu.   \par \par \uc2 The country's economy is slowing, inflation is soaring, a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is slamming corporate profits and hopes of fiscal restraint are faint given national elections due to be held later this year.   \par \par \uc2 "We have to relieve the pressure on monetary policy," he said Tuesday at a seminar in Bucharest, where he also warned that inflation, currently on course to accelerate to 8% next month, has to be tamed this year or the country would have to shelve hopes of adopting the euro in 2014.   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu, one of the longest-serving \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in the world, has been sharing his thoughts to all and sundry in recent days, offering a deluge of commentary to local media and even making a rare appearance on the country's main television show devoted to money matters.   \par \par \uc2 He'll be back on the podium Wednesday, meeting with the heads of Romania's banks, many of whom have complained that the National Bank of Romania has a communications problem.   \par \par \uc2 The agenda isn't public but almost certainly will focus on matters beyond the short-term interest rate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets, according to people in Romania's financial sector.   \par \par \uc2 Erste Bank (EBKOY) Chief Executive Andreas Treichl, Raiffeisen International Bank (RIBH.VI) Chairman Steven van Groningen and Patrick Gelin, CEO of BRD-Societe Generale (BRD.RO) have all made stinging accusations in the local press, arguing that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be open to dialogue rather than prone to issuing diktats.   \par \par \uc2 The claims come as Isarescu, in his various media forays, has signaled that he is mulling the feasibility of new regulatory measures, such as raising reserve requirements for consumer loans, or imposing rules that require credits to be extended only in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by which the borrower receives income.   \par \par \uc2 Such measures are quite likely, said Florin Citu, deputy head of financial markets for  \uc2 ING Groep NV\uc2 's (ING) Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's their favorite way of implementing monetary policy: direct measures of control," he said.   \par \par \uc2 While controversial, such measures are being increasingly tried out in emerging Europe, although mainly in countries such as Croatia, Montenegro or Bulgaria whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are closely tied to hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Such "alternative" measures shouldn't be branded as unorthodox, because the manipulation of reserve requirements is an old tool, one the Bank of Spain has used inside European Monetary Union for mortgage lenders, said Ivailo Vesselinov, an emerging-markets analyst for Dresdner Kleinwort in London.   \par \par \uc2 "The innovation here is really just trying, perhaps out of necessity, to use all available tools," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "But there's nothing to say that the institutions of emerging economies can't lead the way, even if it's not so much setting a trend as it is them being at the appropriate state of the cycle to try these measures," he added. "They could, of course, subsequently be followed by other countries."   \par \par \uc2 One caveat, though, is that the National Bank of Romania is required by statute to target inflation.   \par \par \uc2 As inflation rose to 7.5% in January - up from 3.7% in the spring of 2007 - the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hiked its main interest rate to 9%, up from 7.5% and the highest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in the European Union. But inflation has continued to shoot up and many analysts say more tightening is inevitably on the horizon.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Quandary \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, higher rates risk pushing up the value of the Romanian leu, an effect Isarescu argues could widen the trade and current-account deficits and stoke still more inflationary pressures.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet Isarescu also said Tuesday that letting the leu depreciate is "not the best medicine" for reining in the current-account deficit, which ended 2007 at a whopping 14.3% of gross domestic product, up 66% from the year before. He added that the leu's current rate should prevent further widening of the external deficit.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After rising more than 15% against the euro in the 12 months ending last July, the leu has since given back all that gain and a bit more.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One effect of the slide has been to whack corporate profits, notwithstanding economic growth of more than 5% a year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 35 non-financial companies listed on the Bucharest Stock Exchange posted a net cumulative loss of RON88.6 million (EUR25.4 million) in the fourth quarter of 2007, compared with net cumulative profit of RON280 million (EUR80.4 million) in the same period a year before. The main reason was the soaring value of debt liabilities contracted in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , mostly the euro.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Isarescu has been loquacious of late in warning borrowers to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the leu to avoid risks.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But only one bank - the state-owned CEC or Romanian Savings Bank - has warmly embraced the idea, announcing last week it will stop allowing retail borrowers to be exposed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with national credit growth rates of more than 50% a year, far greater than the increase in deposits, it isn't at all clear how CEC would be able to fund new loans. Most Romanian banks have foreign shareholders and hence strong avenues to access euro-based funding.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the very same reason, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulation restricting lending to leu-based loans would likely bring credit growth to a screeching halt - an outcome that would very likely bring inflation down quickly, albeit at the cost of substantial economic pain.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But while talking up the virtues of the leu, Isarescu is at the same time vocally trying to calm jittery \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by suggesting the current rate is sustainable. Given his frequent reminders that the National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Romania\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can - and sometimes does - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, that message is potent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the message that banks should only lend in RON are two conflicting messages," said ING's Citu, an economist who formerly worked at the Reserve Bank of New Zealand. "The current policy is just creating more problems than they solve."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A policy mistake by the National Bank of Romania at this point could spur a more rapid adoption of the euro as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of choice in private transactions, a nightmare scenario for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Citu.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, Isarescu has competition in the policy space that doesn't make his job any easier.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Varujan Vosganian has been making the rounds, too, publicizing his view that he hopes to stabilize the EUR/RON at around 3.50, up around 5% from the current level and a rate he said would help both importers and exporters.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on television Tuesday, Vosganian also took his chance to upbraid Isarescu for the latter's frequent criticism of Romania's fiscal policy, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "has to analyze its own policy first." The minister added that he himself "never made statements on the bank's policy."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Isarescu has been regularly pleading for the government to refrain from the gigantic wage hikes it has been giving to public-sector workers, which have pushed up prices across the border, more than erased productivity gains and hurt the country's terms of trade and have, above all, fueled a mad rush for more credit as Romanians became optimistic about their future income inside the European Union.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Achieving such restraint won't be easy for a government that lost its parliamentary majority last year and will face voters by November. Indeed, Vosganian has said that Romania is "now in a crisis caused by too much money."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Christopher Emsden, Dow Jones Newswires; +39-02-58-21-99-05; chris.emsden@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Irina Savu in Bucharest contributed to this report.) [ 26-02-08 1733GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 5008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080226e42q0010l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
